/* Periodic Coast - past-exam twins, set B (CHM 11510). Id letter q.
   Original questions modelled on the skills tested in past Purdue CHM 115 exams (Boilerexams practice sets),
   written fresh - no exam text is reproduced. Each record's src names the exam item it twins (e.g.
   "2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q11"). Every stem, number, substance and distractor is new; items that the
   original test-takers often missed (accuracy below 60%) have two twins - one at the same level and one a step
   harder - built around the same trap. Items that needed a figure describe the needed data in words.
   Most of the set sits in gyms 5-7 (thermochemistry, light and quantum theory, configurations and
   periodicity), the material of Evening Exam 2 on Wed Oct 21. Beer's-law items were skipped (not in the
   Fall 2026 course). Every numeric answer and distractor was computed with node from the course constants
   (h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s, c = 2.998 × 10⁸ m/s, N_A = 6.022 × 10²³, Eₙ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J/n², c(water) =
   4.184 J/(g·°C), course atomic masses); each distractor encodes one named mistake. */
chemAddQuestions([
["m1-q-001",1,1,3,"Exam","mcq","A student uses a buret to deliver 25.00 mL of ethanol into a flask. The empty flask weighs 48.217 g and the flask plus ethanol weighs 67.619 g. The accepted density of ethanol is 0.7893 g/mL. Using percent error = (experimental − accepted)/accepted × 100%, what is the percent error of the student’s density?",["−1.67%","−0.0167%","+1.67%","+63.2%","+243%"],0,"Mass of ethanol = 67.619 − 48.217 = 19.402 g. Experimental density = 19.402 g ÷ 25.00 mL = 0.77608 g/mL. Percent error = (0.77608 − 0.7893)/0.7893 × 100% = −1.67% (negative: the measured value is low; the difference of about −0.0132 g/mL carries 3 sig figs). +1.67% reverses the subtraction; −0.0167% forgets × 100; +243% uses the mass of flask plus ethanol; +63.2% computes volume ÷ mass.",["First find the experimental density: mass of the liquid alone ÷ volume.","Mass by difference; then (experimental − accepted)/accepted × 100.","(0.7761 − 0.7893)/0.7893 × 100."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q9"}],
["m1-q-002",1,1,2,"Exam","mcq","A 100 mL graduated cylinder holds 42.5 mL of water. An 87.31 g metal bolt is lowered in, and the water level rises to 53.6 mL. What is the density of the bolt?",["0.127 g/mL","0.909 g/mL","1.63 g/mL","2.05 g/mL","7.87 g/mL"],4,"The bolt’s volume is the volume of water it displaces: 53.6 − 42.5 = 11.1 mL (3 sig figs). Density = 87.31 g ÷ 11.1 mL = 7.87 g/mL (3 sig figs, set by the volume). 1.63 divides by the final reading; 2.05 by the initial reading; 0.127 is volume ÷ mass; 0.909 adds the two readings.",["What volume does the bolt itself occupy?","Volume of solid = final reading − initial reading; d = m/V.","87.31 ÷ 11.1."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q10"}],
["m1-q-003",1,1,3,"Exam","mcq","A garden hose fills a 12-gallon tub in 4.0 minutes. What is the flow rate in millilitres per second? (1 gal = 3.785 L)",["0.19 mL/s","3.2 mL/s","13 mL/s","1.9 × 10² mL/s","1.1 × 10⁴ mL/s"],3,"12 gal × (3.785 L/1 gal) × (1000 mL/1 L) = 45420 mL. 4.0 min × (60 s/1 min) = 240 s. Rate = 45420 mL ÷ 240 s = 189.3 mL/s → 1.9 × 10² mL/s (2 sig figs from 12 and 4.0). 1.1 × 10⁴ mL/s stays per minute; 13 mL/s divides by 3.785 instead of multiplying; 0.19 mL/s is litres per second; 3.2 mL/s converts minutes as if they were hours.",["Convert the volume and the time separately.","gal → L → mL on top; min → s on the bottom.","12 × 3785 mL ÷ 240 s."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q13"}],
["m1-q-004",1,1,2,"Exam","mcq","Ethyl acetate, a solvent in some glues, has a density of 0.902 g/mL. What volume does 58.4 g of ethyl acetate occupy?",["0.0154 mL","52.7 mL","58.4 mL","59.3 mL","64.7 mL"],4,"d = m/V, so V = m/d = 58.4 g ÷ 0.902 g/mL = 64.7 mL. A liquid less dense than water takes up MORE millilitres than its mass in grams - a quick check. 52.7 mL multiplies instead of dividing; 58.4 mL assumes a density of 1.00 g/mL; 0.0154 mL inverts the ratio; 59.3 mL adds the numbers.",["Rearrange d = m/V for V.","V = m/d; check the units cancel to mL.","58.4 ÷ 0.902."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q17"}],
["m2-q-001",2,2,2,"Exam","mcq","Which atom has the same number of neutrons as iron-56 (⁵⁶₂₆Fe)?",["⁵⁶₂₅Mn","⁵⁶₂₇Co","⁵⁸₂₈Ni","⁵⁹₂₇Co","⁵⁵₂₆Fe"],2,"Neutrons = A − Z. Fe-56: 56 − 26 = 30. Ni-58: 58 − 28 = 30 ✓. Mn-56: 31; Co-56: 29; Co-59: 32; Fe-55: 29. Having the same mass number (Mn-56, Co-56) or the same element (Fe-55) does not mean the same neutron count.",["Neutrons = mass number − atomic number.","Fe-56 has 56 − 26 = 30 neutrons; compute A − Z for each choice.","Look for A − Z = 30."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q5"}],
["m2-q-002",2,3,2,"Exam","mcq","What is the correct formula for nickel(II) phosphate?",["Ni₃(PO₄)₂","NiPO₄","Ni₂(PO₄)₃","Ni₃(PO₃)₂","Ni(PO₄)₂"],0,"Nickel(II) is Ni²⁺; phosphate is PO₄³⁻. The charges balance with three Ni²⁺ (+6) and two PO₄³⁻ (−6): Ni₃(PO₄)₂. NiPO₄ would be nickel(III) phosphate; Ni₂(PO₄)₃ swaps the subscripts; PO₃³⁻ is phosphite, not phosphate; Ni(PO₄)₂ has a net charge of −4.",["Write the two ions with their charges.","Ni²⁺ and PO₄³⁻: find the smallest whole numbers that make the total charge zero.","3 × (+2) = 2 × (−3) in magnitude."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q8"}],
["m3-q-001",3,3,2,"Exam","mcq","How many moles of heptane, C₇H₁₆, are in 86.0 g of heptane? Report the answer with the correct number of significant figures.",["0.86 mol","0.858 mol","0.8583 mol","1.17 mol","7.16 mol"],1,"Molar mass C₇H₁₆ = 7(12.01) + 16(1.008) = 100.20 g/mol. n = 86.0 g ÷ 100.20 g/mol = 0.85830 mol → 0.858 mol, because 86.0 g has 3 significant figures. 0.8583 keeps too many digits and 0.86 too few; 1.17 divides molar mass by mass; 7.16 uses carbon’s atomic mass alone.",["Find the molar mass from the formula.","mol = g ÷ g/mol; the answer keeps the sig figs of 86.0 g.","86.0 ÷ 100.20."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q1"}],
["m3-q-002",3,3,3,"Exam","mcq","How many hydrogen atoms are in 0.450 g of aluminum borohydride, Al(BH₄)₃?",["7.55 × 10⁻² H atoms","3.79 × 10²¹ H atoms","1.14 × 10²² H atoms","1.52 × 10²² H atoms","4.55 × 10²² H atoms"],4,"Molar mass Al(BH₄)₃ = 26.98 + 3(10.81 + 4 × 1.008) = 71.51 g/mol. Moles = 0.450 g ÷ 71.51 = 6.293 × 10⁻³ mol. Each formula unit has 3 × 4 = 12 H atoms: 6.293 × 10⁻³ × 12 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 4.55 × 10²² H atoms. 1.52 × 10²² counts the 4 in BH₄ but not the 3 outside the parentheses; 1.14 × 10²² counts only the 3; 3.79 × 10²¹ is formula units, not H atoms; 7.55 × 10⁻² is moles of H, never multiplied by Avogadro’s number.",["grams → moles of compound → atoms of H.","H atoms per formula unit = 3 × 4 = 12.","6.29 × 10⁻³ mol × 12 × 6.022 × 10²³."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q6"}],
["m3-q-003",3,3,4,"Trap","mcq","How many nitrogen atoms are in 1.25 g of magnesium nitrate hexahydrate, Mg(NO₃)₂·6H₂O?",["9.75 × 10⁻³ N atoms","2.94 × 10²¹ N atoms","5.87 × 10²¹ N atoms","1.01 × 10²² N atoms","1.76 × 10²² N atoms"],2,"The molar mass must include the water of hydration: Mg(NO₃)₂ = 24.31 + 2(14.01 + 3 × 16.00) = 148.33; 6H₂O = 6 × 18.016 = 108.10; total 256.43 g/mol. Moles = 1.25 ÷ 256.43 = 4.875 × 10⁻³ mol; each unit has 2 N: 4.875 × 10⁻³ × 2 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 5.87 × 10²¹ N atoms. 1.01 × 10²² leaves the 6H₂O out of the molar mass; 2.94 × 10²¹ counts one N per unit; 1.76 × 10²² counts O atoms in the nitrates (6) instead of N; 9.75 × 10⁻³ stops at moles of N.",["The ·6H₂O is part of every formula unit - include it in the molar mass.","Moles of hydrate × 2 N per unit × 6.022 × 10²³.","M = 256.4 g/mol."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q6"}],
["m3-q-004",3,4,3,"Exam","mcq","Pentane, C₅H₁₂(l), burns completely in excess oxygen to CO₂(g) and H₂O(g) (equation not yet balanced). How many moles of CO₂ form when 145 g of pentane burns?",["0.402 mol","2.01 mol","10.0 mol","12.1 mol","16.5 mol"],2,"Balanced: C₅H₁₂ + 8O₂ → 5CO₂ + 6H₂O, so 1 mol pentane → 5 mol CO₂. Molar mass C₅H₁₂ = 5(12.01) + 12(1.008) = 72.15 g/mol; 145 g ÷ 72.15 = 2.010 mol. CO₂ = 2.010 × 5 = 10.0 mol. 2.01 mol is the pentane itself (no mole ratio); 12.1 mol uses the coefficient of water; 0.402 mol flips the ratio; 16.5 mol divides by the molar mass of CO₂ instead of pentane.",["Balance the combustion equation, carbon first.","g pentane → mol pentane → × (5 mol CO₂ / 1 mol C₅H₁₂).","2.010 × 5."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q34"}],
["m3-q-005",3,4,3,"Exam","mcq","Propane burns according to the balanced equation C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. If 22.0 g of propane and 64.0 g of O₂ react, how many grams of CO₂ can form?",["22.0 g","38.4 g","52.8 g","65.9 g","88.0 g"],2,"Moles: C₃H₈ = 22.0 ÷ 44.09 = 0.4989 mol; O₂ = 64.0 ÷ 32.00 = 2.000 mol. CO₂ each could make: from propane 0.4989 × 3 = 1.497 mol; from O₂ 2.000 × 3/5 = 1.200 mol. O₂ makes less, so it is limiting even though its mass is larger: 1.200 mol × 44.01 g/mol = 52.8 g CO₂. 65.9 g treats propane (the smaller mass) as limiting; 88.0 g skips the 3/5 ratio; 38.4 g applies the mole ratio to grams; 22.0 g forgets the 3 in front of CO₂.",["Convert both reactants to moles of the SAME product.","Propane: × 3 mol CO₂/mol. O₂: × 3 mol CO₂/5 mol O₂. The smaller result wins.","1.200 mol CO₂ × 44.01 g/mol."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q2"}],
["m3-q-006",3,4,3,"Exam","mcq","Aluminum carbide reacts with water to release methane. Balance with the smallest whole numbers: __Al₄C₃(s) + __H₂O(l) → __Al(OH)₃(s) + __CH₄(g). The coefficients, in order, are:",["1, 6, 4, 3","1, 12, 4, 1","2, 12, 8, 3","1, 12, 4, 3","1, 4, 4, 3"],3,"Start with the atoms locked in one compound per side. Al: 4 on the left → 4 Al(OH)₃. C: 3 on the left → 3 CH₄. Now O: 4 Al(OH)₃ hold 12 O, so 12 H₂O. Check H: left 12 × 2 = 24; right 4 × 3 + 3 × 4 = 12 + 12 = 24 ✓. Coefficients 1, 12, 4, 3. \"1, 6, 4, 3\" balances H₂O by H in Al(OH)₃ only; \"1, 12, 4, 1\" leaves carbon unbalanced; \"2, 12, 8, 3\" doubles only some species; \"1, 4, 4, 3\" matches the water to aluminum.",["Balance Al and C first - each appears in only one compound per side.","4 Al(OH)₃ and 3 CH₄; then count O to fix the water.","Then check H: 24 on each side."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q7"}],
["m5-q-001",5,7,2,"Exam","mcq","A gas trapped under a piston absorbs 1.24 kJ of heat from a hot plate. As it warms it pushes the piston outward, doing 385 J of work on the surroundings. What is ΔE for the gas?",["−1.63 kJ","−855 J","+855 J","+1.24 kJ","+1.63 kJ"],2,"ΔE = q + w, with signs from the gas’s (the system’s) point of view. Heat flows IN, so q = +1240 J (convert 1.24 kJ to J first). The gas does work ON the surroundings, so w = −385 J. ΔE = 1240 J + (−385 J) = +855 J. +1.63 kJ adds 385 J as if work were done on the gas; −1.63 kJ treats the heat as released; −855 J reverses both signs; +1.24 kJ ignores the work entirely.",["Decide the sign of q and of w separately, from the gas’s point of view.","Heat absorbed → q > 0. Work done BY the gas → w < 0. Then ΔE = q + w.","1240 J − 385 J."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q35"}],
["m5-q-002",5,7,2,"Exam","mcq","The surroundings compress a sample of gas by doing 420 J of work on it. During the compression the gas gives off 690 J of heat. What is the change in internal energy of the gas?",["−1110 J","−270 J","0 J","+270 J","+1110 J"],1,"ΔE = q + w for the gas. It gives off heat, so q = −690 J. Work is done ON it, so w = +420 J. ΔE = −690 J + 420 J = −270 J: it lost more energy as heat than it gained as work. +270 J has both signs reversed; −1110 J treats the work as done BY the gas; +1110 J ignores both signs; 0 J assumes compression and cooling must cancel.",["Which way does the heat flow, and which way is the work done?","Gives off heat → q < 0; work done ON the gas → w > 0.","−690 + 420."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q15"}],
["m5-q-003",5,7,2,"Exam","mcq","A flameless ration heater warms a soldier’s meal: when water is added, magnesium metal in the pouch reacts and the pouch becomes hot. Taking the reaction as the system, which statement is correct?",["ΔH > 0: the food gains heat, so the process is endothermic","ΔH < 0: the reaction absorbs heat, so it is endothermic","ΔH > 0: the reaction releases heat, so it is exothermic","ΔH < 0: the reaction releases heat, so it is exothermic","ΔH = 0: no heat escapes the sealed pouch"],3,"The pouch and food (the surroundings) get hot, so heat flowed OUT of the reacting system: q(system) < 0, and at constant pressure ΔH = q < 0. A process that releases heat is exothermic. \"ΔH > 0 because the food gains heat\" uses the surroundings’ point of view - ΔH always describes the system. \"ΔH > 0 … exothermic\" and \"ΔH < 0 … endothermic\" pair a sign with the wrong word. Sealing the pouch does not stop heat moving from the reaction to the food.",["Did the surroundings (pouch, food) warm up or cool down?","If the surroundings warm, heat left the system: q(system) < 0.","Negative ΔH goes with exothermic."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q36"}],
["m5-q-004",5,8,3,"Trap","mcq","Solid ammonium chloride is stirred into water in a coffee-cup calorimeter, and the temperature of the solution falls from 22.4 °C to 18.9 °C. Taking the dissolving salt as the system, which set of signs is correct?",["q(soln) > 0, q(rxn) < 0, ΔH < 0 (exothermic)","q(soln) < 0, q(rxn) > 0, ΔH > 0 (endothermic)","q(soln) < 0, q(rxn) < 0, ΔH < 0 (exothermic)","q(soln) > 0, q(rxn) > 0, ΔH > 0 (endothermic)","q(soln) < 0, q(rxn) > 0, ΔH < 0 (exothermic)"],1,"The thermometer measures the solution (the surroundings). It cooled, so q(soln) = m·c·ΔT with ΔT = 18.9 − 22.4 = −3.5 °C: q(soln) < 0. Energy is conserved, so q(rxn) = −q(soln) > 0: the dissolving salt absorbed that heat. At constant pressure ΔH = q(rxn) > 0, so the process is endothermic (like a cold pack). Giving q(rxn) the same sign as q(soln) is the classic slip; so is saying \"the solution lost heat, so the process is exothermic\" - the solution is not the system.",["The thermometer sits in the solution: what does a falling temperature say about q(soln)?","q(rxn) = −q(soln) for an insulated calorimeter.","Solution cools → q(soln) negative → q(rxn) positive → ΔH positive."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q36"}],
["m5-q-005",5,7,3,"Exam","mcq","Two copper blocks are pressed together inside an insulated box whose own heat capacity is negligible. Block A is at 95.0 °C. Block B has three times the mass of block A and starts at 15.0 °C. What common temperature do the blocks reach?",["20.0 °C","35.0 °C","40.0 °C","55.0 °C","75.0 °C"],1,"Heat lost by A = heat gained by B, and both are copper, so c cancels: m(Tf − 95.0) + 3m(Tf − 15.0) = 0 → 4Tf = 95.0 + 45.0 = 140.0 → Tf = 35.0 °C. The heavier, colder block \"wins\", so the answer sits closer to 15 °C. 55.0 °C is the plain average (ignores the mass ratio); 75.0 °C weights the HOT block three times instead; 40.0 °C is half the 80.0 °C gap; 20.0 °C is a quarter of the gap, not a temperature on the scale.",["Heat lost by the hot block = heat gained by the cold block.","Same metal, so c cancels: m₁(Tf − T₁) + m₂(Tf − T₂) = 0 with m₂ = 3m₁.","(95.0 + 3 × 15.0)/4."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q37"}],
["m5-q-006",5,7,4,"Exam","mcq","A 45.0 g iron bolt (c = 0.449 J/(g·°C)) at 88.0 °C is clamped against a 30.0 g aluminum plate (c = 0.897 J/(g·°C)) at 12.0 °C inside an insulated container of negligible heat capacity. What is the final temperature of the pair?",["37.4 °C","44.6 °C","50.0 °C","55.4 °C","57.6 °C"],1,"Heat lost by iron = heat gained by aluminum: (45.0)(0.449)(Tf − 88.0) + (30.0)(0.897)(Tf − 12.0) = 0. The heat capacities are m·c = 20.21 J/°C for iron and 26.91 J/°C for aluminum, so Tf = (20.21 × 88.0 + 26.91 × 12.0)/(47.12) = 44.6 °C. Aluminum has the larger m·c, so Tf is pulled below the 50.0 °C average. 57.6 °C weights by mass only (forgets c); 37.4 °C weights by c only (forgets mass); 50.0 °C ignores both; 55.4 °C attaches each heat capacity to the other metal’s starting temperature.",["Two different metals: each piece’s \"pull\" on the final temperature is its m·c.","Set m₁c₁(Tf − T₁) + m₂c₂(Tf − T₂) = 0 and solve for Tf.","Tf = (20.2 × 88.0 + 26.9 × 12.0)/47.1."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q37"}],
["m5-q-007",5,7,2,"Exam","mcq","Five 2.0 kg samples sit side by side in the sun, and each absorbs exactly the same amount of heat. Which sample shows the SMALLEST temperature rise? (Specific heats in J/(g·°C) are in parentheses.)",["aluminum (0.897)","glass (0.84)","silver (0.235)","lead (0.128)","ethanol (2.44)"],4,"q = m·c·ΔT, so ΔT = q/(m·c). With q and m the same for every sample, the one with the LARGEST specific heat changes temperature the least: ethanol, c = 2.44 J/(g·°C). Lead, with the smallest c, heats up the most - nearly 20 times as much as ethanol. Picking lead answers \"largest rise\", the reverse question.",["Rearrange q = m·c·ΔT for ΔT.","ΔT = q/(m·c): same q and m, so ΔT ∝ 1/c.","Largest c → smallest ΔT."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q38"}],
["m5-q-008",5,7,2,"Exam","mcq","A 0.750 kg copper bar (c = 0.385 J/(g·K)) cools from 412 K to 301 K. What is q for the copper bar?",["−119 kJ","−86.9 kJ","−32.1 kJ","−0.0321 kJ","+32.1 kJ"],2,"q = m·c·ΔT with m in grams: 0.750 kg = 750 g, ΔT = 301 K − 412 K = −111 K. q = (750 g)(0.385 J/(g·K))(−111 K) = −32050 J = −32.1 kJ. The bar loses heat, so its q is negative. +32.1 kJ is the heat the surroundings gain; −119 kJ and −86.9 kJ multiply by one of the temperatures instead of by ΔT; −0.0321 kJ leaves the mass in kilograms when c is per gram.",["From the bar’s point of view: did it gain or lose heat?","q = m·c·ΔT, with m in grams to match c and ΔT = final − initial.","750 × 0.385 × (−111) J, then ÷ 1000."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q14"}],
["m5-q-009",5,7,2,"Exam","mcq","Each reaction below runs at constant pressure and constant temperature. In which one is essentially no pressure-volume work done by or on the system?",["2CO(g) + O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g)","H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g)","CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)","N₂O₄(g) → 2NO₂(g)","2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2SO₃(g)"],1,"At constant P and T, w = −PΔV, and the volume changes only if the number of moles of GAS changes (solids barely change volume). Count gas moles: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl goes 2 → 2, Δn(gas) = 0, so ΔV ≈ 0 and w ≈ 0. 2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂ (3 → 2) and 2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃ (3 → 2) shrink, so work is done ON the system; CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (0 → 1) and N₂O₄ → 2NO₂ (1 → 2) expand, so the system does work. Counting the solids as moles of gas is the usual slip.",["Work at constant pressure is w = −PΔV.","ΔV comes from a change in the number of moles of gas; ignore solids.","Find the reaction with the same number of gas moles on both sides."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q16"}],
["m5-q-010",5,8,3,"Exam","mcq","In lab, 45.20 g of 1.000 M HNO₃ (d = 1.00 g/mL) is poured into 55.40 g of 1.000 M KOH (d = 1.00 g/mL) in a coffee-cup calorimeter. The temperature rises from 21.15 °C to 27.58 °C. Treating the solution like water (c = 4.184 J/(g·°C)) and ignoring the cup, what is ΔH for HNO₃(aq) + KOH(aq) → KNO₃(aq) + H₂O(l), per mole of HNO₃?",["−59.9 kJ/mol","−48.9 kJ/mol","−26.9 kJ/mol","−2.71 kJ/mol","+59.9 kJ/mol"],0,"ΔT = 27.58 − 21.15 = 6.43 °C. The solution is BOTH portions: m = 45.20 + 55.40 = 100.60 g. q(soln) = (100.60 g)(4.184)(6.43) = 2706 J, so q(rxn) = −2706 J. Moles: HNO₃ = 45.20 mL × 1.000 mol/L = 0.04520 mol; KOH = 0.05540 mol, so HNO₃ is limiting. ΔH = −2706 J ÷ 0.04520 mol = −59.9 kJ/mol. −48.9 divides by the excess KOH; −26.9 uses only the acid’s mass for the solution; −2.71 never divides by moles; +59.9 gives q(rxn) the sign of q(soln).",["Find q(soln) first, then q(rxn) = −q(soln).","The solution mass is the sum of both portions; the moles come from the LIMITING reactant (mL × M).","q(soln) ≈ 100.60 × 4.184 × 6.43 J; divide −q by 0.04520 mol."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q39"}],
["m5-q-011",5,8,4,"Exam","mcq","In a calorimeter whose own heat capacity is 12.5 J/°C, 60.0 mL of 0.800 M HCl is mixed with 40.0 mL of 1.10 M NaOH, both at 22.40 °C. The temperature peaks at 28.12 °C. Take the solution’s density as 1.00 g/mL and its specific heat as 4.184 J/(g·°C). What is ΔH of neutralization per mole of water formed?",["−56.0 kJ/mol","−54.4 kJ/mol","−51.3 kJ/mol","−2.46 kJ/mol","+56.0 kJ/mol"],0,"Moles: HCl = 0.0600 L × 0.800 M = 0.0480 mol; NaOH = 0.0400 L × 1.10 M = 0.0440 mol. NaOH is limiting, so 0.0440 mol H₂O forms. Heat absorbed by solution AND cup: q = (100.0 g × 4.184 + 12.5 J/°C)(28.12 − 22.40) = (430.9 J/°C)(5.72 °C) = 2465 J. q(rxn) = −2465 J, and ΔH = −2465 J ÷ 0.0440 mol = −56.0 kJ/mol. −51.3 divides by the HCl in excess; −54.4 leaves out the cup’s 12.5 J/°C; −2.46 is q(rxn) in kJ, not per mole; the positive value has the wrong sign for a reaction that warmed its surroundings.",["Which reactant runs out? That fixes the moles of water.","Total heat absorbed = (m·c + C(cal))·ΔT; q(rxn) is minus that.","NaOH: 0.0440 mol. q = (418.4 + 12.5)(5.72) J."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q39"}],
["m5-q-012",5,8,3,"Exam","mcq","A student dissolves 3.150 g of LiCl(s) in 60.00 g of water at 21.30 °C in a coffee-cup calorimeter. The highest temperature reached is 31.68 °C. Assuming the solution’s specific heat is 4.184 J/(g·°C) and the cup absorbs no heat, what is ΔH of solution of LiCl in kJ/mol?",["−3.69 × 10⁴ kJ/mol","−36.9 kJ/mol","−35.1 kJ/mol","−2.74 kJ/mol","+36.9 kJ/mol"],1,"The dissolved salt is part of the solution, so m = 60.00 + 3.150 = 63.15 g. ΔT = 31.68 − 21.30 = 10.38 °C. q(soln) = (63.15)(4.184)(10.38) = 2743 J, so q(rxn) = −2743 J. Moles LiCl = 3.150 g ÷ 42.39 g/mol = 0.07431 mol. ΔH = −2.743 kJ ÷ 0.07431 mol = −36.9 kJ/mol (exothermic: the water warmed). −35.1 uses the water’s mass alone; −2.74 forgets to divide by moles; −3.69 × 10⁴ is the answer in J/mol labelled kJ/mol; the positive value has the sign of q(soln).",["What mass actually warms up - just the water?","m = water + salt; q(rxn) = −m·c·ΔT; divide by moles of LiCl (M = 42.39 g/mol).","q(soln) = 63.15 × 4.184 × 10.38 J."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q40"}],
["m5-q-013",5,8,4,"Exam","mcq","An instant cold pack is modelled in lab: 4.80 g of NH₄NO₃(s) is dissolved in 75.00 g of water at 22.85 °C in a coffee-cup calorimeter, and the temperature drops to 18.24 °C. With c = 4.184 J/(g·°C) for the solution and no heat absorbed by the cup, what is ΔH of solution of NH₄NO₃ (kJ/mol)?",["−25.7 kJ/mol","+0.321 kJ/mol","+1.54 kJ/mol","+24.1 kJ/mol","+25.7 kJ/mol"],4,"ΔT = 18.24 − 22.85 = −4.61 °C. Solution mass = 75.00 + 4.80 = 79.80 g. q(soln) = (79.80)(4.184)(−4.61) = −1539 J; the water LOST heat, so q(rxn) = +1539 J. Molar mass NH₄NO₃ = 80.05 g/mol, n = 4.80/80.05 = 0.05996 mol. ΔH = +1.539 kJ ÷ 0.05996 mol = +25.7 kJ/mol: endothermic, which is why the pack gets cold. −25.7 copies the sign of q(soln); +24.1 uses only the water’s mass; +1.54 is the total heat, not per mole; +0.321 is per gram, not per mole.",["The temperature FELL: what sign does q(rxn) have?","q(rxn) = −(m_water + m_salt)·c·ΔT; then divide by moles of NH₄NO₃ (80.05 g/mol).","q(rxn) = +1539 J for 0.0600 mol."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q40"}],
["m5-q-014",5,8,2,"Exam","mcq","Pellets of NaOH(s) are dropped into room-temperature water in a coffee-cup calorimeter, and the solution becomes noticeably warmer. Taking the dissolving NaOH as the system, which description is correct?",["exothermic, with q(rxn) > 0","endothermic, with q(rxn) < 0","endothermic, with q(rxn) > 0","exothermic, with q(rxn) < 0","exothermic, with q(rxn) = 0 because the cup is insulated"],3,"The solution (surroundings) warms, so q(soln) > 0. The heat came from the dissolving process, so q(rxn) = −q(soln) < 0: heat released, exothermic. \"exothermic, with q(rxn) > 0\" gives q(rxn) the sign of q(soln) - the most common slip. The insulated cup keeps the heat inside the solution; it does not make q(rxn) zero.",["Warmer solution → which way did heat flow?","q(rxn) = −q(soln).","Heat leaves the system: exothermic, q(rxn) negative."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q41"}],
["m5-q-015",5,8,3,"Trap","mcq","During a reaction in a coffee-cup calorimeter, the temperature of the solution falls from 24.6 °C to 19.8 °C. Which statement is TRUE?",["The solution lost heat, so q(soln) < 0 and q(rxn) > 0.","The reaction released heat, which is why the temperature changed.","q(soln) and q(rxn) are both negative, because the temperature fell.","The reaction is exothermic, because the solution lost heat.","q(rxn) = q(soln), because the insulated cup keeps all the heat inside."],0,"The solution cooled, so q(soln) = m·c·ΔT with ΔT = −4.8 °C is negative. That heat went INTO the reacting system: q(rxn) = −q(soln) > 0, an endothermic reaction. A reaction that released heat would have warmed the solution; both q values being negative would break energy conservation; \"exothermic because the solution lost heat\" judges the system by the surroundings; and the cup’s insulation makes q(rxn) = −q(soln), not +q(soln).",["Separate the system (reaction) from the surroundings (solution).","Insulated calorimeter: q(rxn) + q(soln) = 0.","Falling temperature → q(soln) < 0 → q(rxn) > 0."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q41"}],
["m5-q-016",5,8,3,"Exam","mcq","Magnesium ribbon burns with a dazzling flame: 2Mg(s) + O₂(g) → 2MgO(s), ΔH = −1203 kJ. What is ΔH when 0.150 kg of magnesium burns completely?",["−1.80 × 10⁵ kJ","−7.42 × 10³ kJ","−3.71 × 10³ kJ","−3.71 kJ","+3.71 × 10³ kJ"],2,"0.150 kg = 150 g Mg; 150 g ÷ 24.31 g/mol = 6.170 mol Mg. The −1203 kJ belongs to 2 mol Mg, so ΔH = 6.170 mol × (−1203 kJ / 2 mol Mg) = −3711 kJ ≈ −3.71 × 10³ kJ. −7.42 × 10³ kJ treats −1203 kJ as per mole of Mg (ignores the coefficient 2); −3.71 kJ uses 0.150 g; −1.80 × 10⁵ kJ multiplies grams by ΔH without converting to moles; the positive value forgets that burning releases heat.",["ΔH is for the equation as written - how many moles of Mg is that?","kg → g → mol Mg, then × (−1203 kJ / 2 mol Mg).","6.170 mol × (−601.5 kJ/mol)."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q1"}],
["m5-q-017",5,8,4,"Exam","mcq","Ammonia is oxidized in the first step of nitric acid manufacture: 4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g), ΔH = −906 kJ. What is ΔH when 1.25 kg of O₂ is consumed?",["−3.54 × 10⁴ kJ","−8.85 × 10³ kJ","−7.08 × 10³ kJ","−7.08 kJ","+7.08 × 10³ kJ"],2,"1.25 kg O₂ = 1250 g ÷ 32.00 g/mol = 39.06 mol O₂. The equation pairs −906 kJ with 5 mol O₂: ΔH = 39.06 × (−906 kJ / 5 mol O₂) = −7078 kJ ≈ −7.08 × 10³ kJ. −8.85 × 10³ kJ uses the coefficient of NH₃ (4) instead of O₂ (5); −3.54 × 10⁴ kJ treats −906 kJ as per mole of O₂; −7.08 kJ forgets kg → g; the positive value drops the sign.",["Use the coefficient of the substance you were given, O₂.","kg → g → mol O₂ → × (−906 kJ / 5 mol O₂).","39.06 × (−181.2) kJ."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q1"}],
["m5-q-018",5,9,3,"Exam","mcq","Use the data: (1) N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g), ΔH = −91.8 kJ; (2) N₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO(g), ΔH = +180.6 kJ; (3) 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(g), ΔH = −483.6 kJ. Calculate ΔH for 4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g).",["−1273.2 kJ","−906.0 kJ","−422.4 kJ","+61.2 kJ","+906.0 kJ"],1,"NH₃ must be a reactant, so reverse (1) and double it: 4NH₃ → 2N₂ + 6H₂, ΔH = +183.6 kJ. Double (2): 2N₂ + 2O₂ → 4NO, ΔH = +361.2 kJ. Triple (3): 6H₂ + 3O₂ → 6H₂O, ΔH = −1450.8 kJ. Adding cancels 2N₂ and 6H₂ and gives 4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 4NO + 6H₂O with ΔH = 183.6 + 361.2 − 1450.8 = −906.0 kJ. −1273.2 kJ doubles (1) without reversing it; +61.2 kJ forgets to scale (3); −422.4 kJ scales (3) by 2 instead of 3; +906.0 kJ has every sign flipped.",["Match each target substance: NH₃ is a reactant, NO and H₂O are products.","Reverse and ×2 equation (1), ×2 equation (2), ×3 equation (3).","+183.6 + 361.2 − 1450.8."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q2"}],
["m5-q-019",5,9,2,"Exam","mcq","For which reaction is ΔH°rxn equal to the standard enthalpy of formation of calcium carbonate, CaCO₃(s)?",["CaO(s) + CO₂(g) → CaCO₃(s)","Ca(s) + C(diamond) + 3/2 O₂(g) → CaCO₃(s)","2Ca(s) + 2C(graphite) + 3O₂(g) → 2CaCO₃(s)","Ca(s) + C(graphite) + 3O(g) → CaCO₃(s)","Ca(s) + C(graphite) + 3/2 O₂(g) → CaCO₃(s)"],4,"A formation reaction makes exactly ONE mole of the compound from its elements in their standard states (Ca(s), C(graphite), O₂(g)) - fractional coefficients are allowed. CaO + CO₂ starts from compounds, not elements; C(diamond) and O(g) are not the standard states of carbon and oxygen; the doubled equation makes 2 mol, so its ΔH is 2ΔH°f.",["Formation: elements → one mole of compound.","Every reactant must be an element in its standard state at 25 °C and 1 atm.","Graphite and O₂(g), with a coefficient of 1 on CaCO₃."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q3"}],
["m5-q-020",5,9,3,"Exam","mcq","Silane, SiH₄(g), ignites in air to form SiO₂(s) and liquid water. Using ΔH°f values (kJ/mol) SiH₄(g) +34.3, SiO₂(s) −910.9, H₂O(l) −285.8, H₂O(g) −241.8, calculate ΔH°rxn for the combustion of one mole of silane.",["−1516.8 kJ","−1448.2 kJ","−1428.8 kJ","−1231.0 kJ","+1516.8 kJ"],0,"Balance first: SiH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → SiO₂(s) + 2H₂O(l). ΔH° = ΣnΔH°f(products) − ΣmΔH°f(reactants) = [(−910.9) + 2(−285.8)] − [(+34.3) + 2(0)] = −1516.8 kJ. −1231.0 kJ forgets the 2 on water; −1448.2 kJ adds the reactant instead of subtracting it; −1428.8 kJ uses the H₂O(g) value although the water is liquid; +1516.8 kJ is reactants − products.",["Write and balance the equation before touching the table.","Products − reactants, each ΔH°f times its coefficient; O₂ is 0. Read the phase of water.","−910.9 + 2(−285.8) − 34.3."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q4"}],
["m5-q-021",5,9,3,"Exam","mcq","Given: (1) C₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(l), ΔH° = −2220 kJ; (2) H₂O(l) → H₂O(g), ΔH° = +44.0 kJ. What is ΔH° for C₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(g)?",["−2396 kJ","−2264 kJ","−2176 kJ","−2044 kJ","+2044 kJ"],3,"Keep (1) as written, then turn its 4 mol of liquid water into vapor by adding 4 × (2): 4H₂O(l) → 4H₂O(g), ΔH = 4(+44.0) = +176 kJ. Total ΔH° = −2220 + 176 = −2044 kJ. Making steam instead of liquid water releases LESS heat, because some energy stays in the vapor. −2396 kJ subtracts the vaporization; −2176 kJ and −2264 kJ use (2) only once; +2044 kJ flips the sign.",["The only difference between the two equations is the phase of the water.","Add 4 × (H₂O(l) → H₂O(g)) to equation (1).","−2220 + 4(44.0)."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q5"}],
["m5-q-022",5,9,3,"Exam","mcq","Ethane burns according to 2C₂H₆(g) + 7O₂(g) → 4CO₂(g) + 6H₂O(g). Using ΔH°f (kJ/mol): C₂H₆(g) −84.7, CO₂(g) −393.5, H₂O(g) −241.8, H₂O(l) −285.8, find the heat of combustion per mole of ethane.",["−2855.4 kJ/mol","−1597.1 kJ/mol","−1559.7 kJ/mol","−1427.7 kJ/mol","+1427.7 kJ/mol"],3,"For the equation as written: ΔH° = [4(−393.5) + 6(−241.8)] − [2(−84.7) + 7(0)] = −3024.8 − (−169.4) = −2855.4 kJ. That is for 2 mol of C₂H₆, so per mole: −2855.4 ÷ 2 = −1427.7 kJ/mol. −2855.4 forgets to divide by 2; −1559.7 uses the liquid-water value although the equation makes H₂O(g); −1597.1 adds the ethane term instead of subtracting it; the positive value has the sign reversed.",["ΔH from the table belongs to the equation as written - how many moles of ethane is that?","Products − reactants with coefficients, H₂O(g) value, then ÷ 2.","(−3024.8 + 169.4)/2."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q6"}],
["m5-q-023",5,9,4,"Exam","mcq","Methanol is burned as a racing fuel: 2CH₃OH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(l). With ΔH°f (kJ/mol) CH₃OH(l) −238.6, CO₂(g) −393.5, H₂O(l) −285.8, H₂O(g) −241.8, how much heat is released when 10.0 g of methanol burns?",["199 kJ","227 kJ","301 kJ","453 kJ","727 kJ"],1,"ΔH° for the equation (2 mol CH₃OH) = [2(−393.5) + 4(−285.8)] − [2(−238.6)] = −1453.0 kJ, so −726.50 kJ per mole of methanol. Molar mass CH₃OH = 32.042 g/mol; 10.0 g = 0.3121 mol. Heat released = 0.3121 × 726.50 kJ = 227 kJ. 453 kJ uses the 2-mol ΔH as if it were per mole; 199 kJ uses H₂O(g) although the water is liquid; 727 kJ is the per-mole value (never converted 10.0 g); 301 kJ leaves methanol’s own ΔH°f out.",["Three stages: ΔH° for the equation, then per mole of CH₃OH, then for 10.0 g.","Products − reactants (liquid water), ÷ 2, then × (10.0 g ÷ 32.04 g/mol).","−726.5 kJ/mol × 0.312 mol."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q6"}],
["m5-q-024",5,9,2,"Exam","mcq","Given Mg(s) + ½O₂(g) → MgO(s), ΔH = −601.6 kJ, and Mg(OH)₂(s) → MgO(s) + H₂O(l), ΔH = +37.1 kJ, calculate ΔH for Mg(s) + ½O₂(g) + H₂O(l) → Mg(OH)₂(s).",["−564.5 kJ","+638.7 kJ","−638.7 kJ","+564.5 kJ","It cannot be found without ΔH°f of H₂O(l)"],2,"Keep the first equation (it supplies Mg + ½O₂ on the left). Reverse the second so Mg(OH)₂ is a product: MgO(s) + H₂O(l) → Mg(OH)₂(s), ΔH = −37.1 kJ. Adding cancels MgO: ΔH = −601.6 + (−37.1) = −638.7 kJ. −564.5 kJ forgets to change the sign of the reversed equation; the positive answers flip the whole result; no extra data is needed because Hess’s law only needs equations that add up to the target.",["Which equation has Mg(OH)₂ on the wrong side?","Reverse the second equation (sign changes) and add it to the first.","−601.6 − 37.1."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q20"}],
["m6-q-001",6,10,2,"Exam","mcq","A violet-blue line in a discharge lamp has a frequency of 7.15 × 10¹⁴ Hz. What is its wavelength in nanometres?",["4.19 × 10⁻¹⁶ nm","4.19 × 10⁻⁷ nm","4.19 × 10² nm","2.38 × 10⁶ nm","2.14 × 10²³ nm"],2,"λ = c/ν = (2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)/(7.15 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹) = 4.19 × 10⁻⁷ m. Convert: 4.19 × 10⁻⁷ m × (10⁹ nm / 1 m) = 419 nm = 4.19 × 10² nm, inside the visible range as it should be. 4.19 × 10⁻⁷ \"nm\" is the metre value with the wrong unit; 4.19 × 10⁻¹⁶ nm multiplies by 10⁻⁹ instead of 10⁹; 2.38 × 10⁶ nm inverts the formula (ν/c); 2.14 × 10²³ nm multiplies c by ν.",["c = λν, so λ = c/ν.","The answer comes out in metres; 1 m = 10⁹ nm.","2.998 × 10⁸ ÷ 7.15 × 10¹⁴ = 4.19 × 10⁻⁷ m."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q7"}],
["m6-q-002",6,10,2,"Exam","mcq","Which list puts these kinds of radiation in order from HIGHEST frequency (left) to LOWEST frequency (right)?",["radio; microwave; infrared; ultraviolet; gamma","gamma; infrared; ultraviolet; microwave; radio","gamma; ultraviolet; infrared; microwave; radio","ultraviolet; gamma; infrared; radio; microwave","gamma; ultraviolet; infrared; radio; microwave"],2,"Highest frequency = shortest wavelength = highest energy. From the short-wavelength end: gamma (< 0.01 nm) > ultraviolet (10-400 nm) > infrared (700 nm-1 mm) > microwave (mm-cm) > radio (metres and up). \"radio; microwave; …; gamma\" is the same list backwards (lowest to highest). Swapping ultraviolet and infrared, or microwave and radio, breaks the order: UV is on the violet side of visible, IR on the red side, and radio waves are the longest of all.",["Highest frequency means shortest wavelength.","Gamma rays are the SHORT-wavelength end; radio waves are the long end.","Ultraviolet comes before infrared; microwave before radio."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q8"}],
["m6-q-003",6,10,2,"Trap","mcq","Arrange visible light, X-rays, microwaves, ultraviolet and infrared in order of INCREASING wavelength (shortest first).",["X-ray < ultraviolet < visible < infrared < microwave","microwave < infrared < visible < ultraviolet < X-ray","X-ray < infrared < visible < ultraviolet < microwave","ultraviolet < X-ray < visible < infrared < microwave","X-ray < ultraviolet < infrared < visible < microwave"],0,"Shortest wavelength first: X-rays (about 0.01-10 nm) < ultraviolet (10-400 nm) < visible (400-700 nm) < infrared (700 nm-1 mm) < microwave (mm-cm). \"microwave < … < X-ray\" is decreasing wavelength - that is the order of increasing ENERGY, and mixing the two up is exactly the recitation-sheet mistake. UV and IR sit on opposite sides of visible: ultra-violet beyond violet (shorter), infra-red below red (longer).",["Shortest wavelength = most energetic. Which of these is most energetic?","Ultraviolet is just shorter than violet; infrared is just longer than red.","X-ray first, microwave last."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q8"}],
["m6-q-004",6,10,3,"Exam","mcq","A Blu-ray laser emits violet light of wavelength 405 nm. What is the energy of one mole of these photons, in kJ/mol?",["8.14 × 10⁻⁴⁶ kJ/mol","4.90 × 10⁻¹⁹ kJ/mol","2.95 × 10⁻⁷ kJ/mol","295 kJ/mol","2.95 × 10⁵ kJ/mol"],3,"Per photon: E = hc/λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)(2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)/(405 × 10⁻⁹ m) = 4.905 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Per mole: × 6.022 × 10²³ = 2.954 × 10⁵ J/mol = 295 kJ/mol. 4.90 × 10⁻¹⁹ is the energy of ONE photon in J, not a molar value; 2.95 × 10⁵ is J/mol written as kJ/mol; 2.95 × 10⁻⁷ leaves λ in nm; 8.14 × 10⁻⁴⁶ divides by Avogadro’s number instead of multiplying.",["Two steps: energy of one photon, then of a mole of photons.","E = hc/λ with λ in metres; then × N_A and ÷ 1000.","4.90 × 10⁻¹⁹ J × 6.022 × 10²³ ÷ 1000."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q10"}],
["m6-q-005",6,10,4,"Trap","mcq","Light carrying 165 kJ per mole of photons is shone on a sample. What is the wavelength of this light in nanometres?",["1.20 × 10⁻²¹ nm","7.25 × 10⁻⁷ nm","0.725 nm","725 nm","7.25 × 10⁵ nm"],3,"Work PER PHOTON before using λ = hc/E. 165 kJ/mol = 1.65 × 10⁵ J/mol; ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ photons/mol = 2.740 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per photon. λ = hc/E = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸)/(2.740 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 7.25 × 10⁻⁷ m = 725 nm (red light). 7.25 × 10⁻⁷ nm is the metre value mislabelled; 7.25 × 10⁵ nm leaves the energy in kJ; 1.20 × 10⁻²¹ nm puts the per-MOLE energy into hc/E (no ÷ N_A) - the per-atom vs per-mole slip; 0.725 nm converts kJ the wrong way (÷ 1000).",["λ = hc/E needs the energy of ONE photon, in joules.","kJ/mol → J/mol (× 1000) → J/photon (÷ 6.022 × 10²³) → λ = hc/E → nm.","E(photon) = 2.74 × 10⁻¹⁹ J."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q10"}],
["m6-q-006",6,10,2,"Exam","mcq","An AM radio station broadcasts at 1180 kHz. What is the wavelength of its signal?",["3.94 × 10⁻³ m","0.254 m","254 m","2.54 × 10⁵ m","3.54 × 10¹⁴ m"],2,"1180 kHz = 1180 × 10³ Hz = 1.180 × 10⁶ s⁻¹. λ = c/ν = (2.998 × 10⁸ m/s)/(1.180 × 10⁶ s⁻¹) = 254 m - AM waves are hundreds of metres long. 0.254 m treats kHz as MHz; 2.54 × 10⁵ m forgets the \"kilo\" entirely; 3.94 × 10⁻³ m divides ν by c; 3.54 × 10¹⁴ m multiplies them.",["Convert the frequency to hertz (s⁻¹) first.","k = 10³, so 1180 kHz = 1.180 × 10⁶ Hz; λ = c/ν.","2.998 × 10⁸ ÷ 1.180 × 10⁶."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q13"}],
["m6-q-007",6,10,3,"Exam","mcq","Orange light from a neon sign has a wavelength of 612 nm. What is the energy of this light, in kJ per mole of photons?",["5.39 × 10⁻⁴⁶","3.25 × 10⁻¹⁹","1.95 × 10²","1.95 × 10⁵","1.95 × 10⁸"],2,"E(photon) = hc/λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸)/(612 × 10⁻⁹ m) = 3.246 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. × 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.955 × 10⁵ J/mol; ÷ 1000 = 1.95 × 10² kJ/mol. 3.25 × 10⁻¹⁹ stops at one photon; 1.95 × 10⁵ is still in J/mol; 1.95 × 10⁸ multiplies by 1000 when converting J → kJ instead of dividing; 5.39 × 10⁻⁴⁶ divides by Avogadro’s number.",["One photon first, then a mole of them.","E = hc/λ (λ in m), × N_A, then J → kJ.","3.25 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 6.022 × 10²³ / 1000."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q14"}],
["m6-q-008",6,10,4,"Exam","mcq","Breaking the Cl−Cl bond takes 243 kJ/mol. What is the LONGEST wavelength of light whose photons each carry enough energy to break one Cl−Cl bond?",["8.17 × 10⁻²² nm","4.04 × 10⁻¹⁹ nm","492 nm","4.92 × 10⁵ nm","6.09 × 10¹⁴ nm"],2,"One photon breaks one bond, so use the energy per BOND: 243 × 10³ J/mol ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 4.035 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. The longest usable wavelength is the one whose photon has exactly this energy (longer λ means less energy): λ = hc/E = 4.92 × 10⁻⁷ m = 492 nm. 4.92 × 10⁵ nm leaves the energy in kJ; 8.17 × 10⁻²² nm uses the per-mole energy directly; 6.09 × 10¹⁴ is the frequency in Hz, not a wavelength; 4.04 × 10⁻¹⁹ is the photon energy in J.",["One photon must supply the energy for one bond, not one mole of bonds.","kJ/mol → J/bond (× 1000, ÷ N_A), then λ = hc/E.","E = 4.04 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per bond."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q14"}],
["m6-q-009",6,11,2,"Exam","mcq","An electron in hydrogen can drop between any two of the levels n = 1 through n = 5. How many different emission lines can these transitions produce?",["4","5","10","15","20"],2,"Each distinct pair of levels gives one line, whichever way the electron drops: 5 → 4, 3, 2, 1 (4 lines), 4 → 3, 2, 1 (3), 3 → 2, 1 (2) and 2 → 1 (1): 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10 = 5 × 4/2. 4 counts only the drops to n = 1 (or only from n = 5); 5 is the number of levels; 20 counts each pair twice (up and down); 15 is the count for six levels.",["Each emission line corresponds to one pair of levels.","List the drops from the top level down, then from the next level, and so on.","4 + 3 + 2 + 1."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q9"}],
["m6-q-010",6,11,3,"Exam","mcq","A hydrogen atom in the n = 5 level emits a photon of wavelength 434.0 nm. In which level does the electron end up? (Eₙ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J/n²)",["n = 1","n = 2","n = 3","n = 4","n = 6"],1,"Photon energy: E = hc/λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸)/(434.0 × 10⁻⁹) = 4.577 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Emission lowers the atom’s energy by this amount: −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/n_f² − 1/5²) = −4.577 × 10⁻¹⁹, so 1/n_f² = 1/25 + 4.577 × 10⁻¹⁹/2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ = 0.0400 + 0.2100 = 0.2500, n_f² = 4.00, n_f = 2. A 434 nm (visible) line must end on n = 2 - the Balmer series. n = 6 would be absorption, not emission; n = 1 would need an ultraviolet photon; n = 3 or 4 give infrared photons.",["Find the photon’s energy from its wavelength.","Emission: E(photon) = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/n_f² − 1/n_i²). Solve for 1/n_f².","1/n_f² = 0.0400 + 0.210."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q11"}],
["m6-q-011",6,11,4,"Exam","mcq","A hydrogen atom whose electron is in the n = 3 level ABSORBS an infrared photon of wavelength 1094 nm. What is the electron’s new level? (Eₙ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J/n²)",["n = 2","n = 4","n = 5","n = 6","n = 7"],3,"E(photon) = hc/λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)(2.998 × 10⁸)/(1094 × 10⁻⁹) = 1.816 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Absorption RAISES the atom’s energy: E_f − E_i = +1.816 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, so 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/3² − 1/n_f²) = 1.816 × 10⁻¹⁹. Then 1/n_f² = 1/9 − 0.0833 = 0.0278, n_f² = 35.9, n_f = 6. n = 2 treats the photon as emitted, but an absorbed photon can only move the electron up (and the emission arithmetic gives n_f ≈ 2.3, not a whole number); n = 4 and 5 need less energy than the photon carries, n = 7 needs more.",["Absorbing a photon moves the electron UP.","E(photon) = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/n_i² − 1/n_f²) with n_i = 3.","1/n_f² = 0.1111 − 0.0833."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q11"}],
["m6-q-012",6,11,2,"Exam","mcq","Compared with the photon emitted when a hydrogen electron drops from n = 3 to n = 2, the photon emitted in a drop from n = 4 to n = 3 has ______ energy and a ______ wavelength.",["more; shorter","less; shorter","more; longer","less; longer","the same; the same, since each is a one-level drop"],3,"Hydrogen’s levels crowd together as n rises: ΔE(3→2) = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/4 − 1/9) = 3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, but ΔE(4→3) = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/9 − 1/16) = 1.06 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. So the 4 → 3 photon has LESS energy, and since λ = hc/E, a LONGER wavelength (it is infrared; 3 → 2 is red visible light). Energy and wavelength always move in opposite directions, which rules out \"less; shorter\" and \"more; longer\". One-level drops are not equal because the gaps shrink higher up.",["Are the energy levels evenly spaced in hydrogen?","Compare 1/4 − 1/9 with 1/9 − 1/16.","Smaller ΔE → longer λ."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q15"}],
["m6-q-013",6,11,3,"Exam","mcq","What frequency of light will raise a ground-state hydrogen electron to the n = 4 level? (Eₙ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J/n²)",["3.29 × 10¹⁵ Hz","3.08 × 10¹⁵ Hz","2.04 × 10⁻¹⁸ J","97.2 nm","1.35 × 10⁻⁵¹ Hz"],1,"ΔE = E₄ − E₁ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/16 − 1/1) = +2.044 × 10⁻¹⁸ J (absorbed). ν = ΔE/h = 2.044 × 10⁻¹⁸ J ÷ 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s = 3.08 × 10¹⁵ Hz. 3.29 × 10¹⁵ Hz would remove the electron completely (n = ∞, ionization); 2.04 × 10⁻¹⁸ J is the energy, not the frequency asked for; 97.2 nm is the wavelength of that light; 1.35 × 10⁻⁵¹ Hz multiplies by h instead of dividing.",["First find the energy gap between n = 1 and n = 4.","ΔE = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1 − 1/16) J, then ν = ΔE/h.","2.04 × 10⁻¹⁸ J ÷ 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q16"}],
["m6-q-014",6,12,2,"Exam","mcq","For the principal quantum number n = 3, how many d orbitals are there, and how many orbitals are there in total?",["10 d orbitals; 18 orbitals in all","5 d orbitals; 18 orbitals in all","3 d orbitals; 9 orbitals in all","5 d orbitals; 14 orbitals in all","5 d orbitals; 9 orbitals in all"],4,"For n = 3, l can be 0, 1 or 2 (3s, 3p, 3d). A d sublevel (l = 2) has mₗ = −2 … +2, i.e. 5 orbitals. Total = 1 + 3 + 5 = 9 = n². 10 and 18 are ELECTRON counts (2 per orbital); 3 is the number of p orbitals; 14 adds f orbitals, which do not exist until n = 4.",["List the allowed l values for n = 3.","Orbitals per sublevel = 2l + 1; total orbitals in a shell = n².","1 + 3 + 5."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q12"}],
["m6-q-015",6,12,2,"Exam","mcq","Which gives the correct n and l values and the number of orbitals for the 4d sublevel?",["n = 4, l = 3, 7 orbitals","n = 4, l = 2, 5 orbitals","n = 4, l = 2, 10 orbitals","n = 3, l = 2, 5 orbitals","n = 4, l = 1, 3 orbitals"],1,"The number in \"4d\" is n = 4; the letter d means l = 2 (s 0, p 1, d 2, f 3). mₗ runs from −2 to +2, so there are 2l + 1 = 5 orbitals. l = 3 is f; 10 is the number of ELECTRONS a d sublevel holds; n = 3 confuses 4d with the 3d that sits beside it in the d-block; l = 1 is p.",["The number is n; the letter gives l.","s, p, d, f ↔ l = 0, 1, 2, 3; orbitals = 2l + 1.","d → l = 2 → 5 orbitals."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q17"}],
["m6-q-016",6,12,3,"Exam","mcq","An orbital’s radial probability plot shows two maxima separated by one radial node, and the orbital also has one nodal plane passing through the nucleus. Which orbital is it?",["2s","2p","3s","3p","3d"],3,"A nodal plane through the nucleus is an angular node: l = 1, a p orbital. Radial nodes = n − l − 1 = 1, so n − 1 − 1 = 1 → n = 3: a 3p orbital (n − l = 2 maxima in its radial plot). 2s has one radial node but no angular node; 2p has an angular node but no radial node (one maximum); 3s has two radial nodes and no plane; 3d has two angular nodes and no radial node.",["Angular nodes tell you l; radial nodes tell you n − l − 1.","One nodal plane → l = 1. One radial node → n − l − 1 = 1.","l = 1 and n = 3."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q18"}],
["m7-q-001",7,13,2,"Exam","mcq","How many valence electrons and how many core (inner) electrons does a neutral selenium (Se) atom have?",["6 valence, 28 core","16 valence, 18 core","4 valence, 30 core","6 valence, 18 core","6 valence, 34 core"],0,"Se (Z = 34) is [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁴. For a main-group element the valence electrons are the outer-shell s and p electrons: 4s² 4p⁴ = 6 (group 16). Everything else is core: 34 − 6 = 28, which includes the filled 3d¹⁰. \"16 valence\" counts the 3d¹⁰ as valence; \"18 core\" forgets the 3d¹⁰ are core; \"34 core\" counts every electron as core; 4 valence confuses Se with a group 14 element.",["Write the configuration of Se, Z = 34.","Valence = electrons in the highest-n shell (4s and 4p); a filled 3d set is core.","4s² 4p⁴ → 6; 34 − 6 = 28."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q19"}],
["m7-q-002",7,13,2,"Exam","mcq","Which is the correct condensed ground-state electron configuration of a neutral cobalt (Co) atom?",["[Ar] 3d⁹","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁸","[Kr] 4s² 3d⁷","[Ar] 4s² 4d⁷","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁷"],4,"Co has Z = 27. After [Ar] (18 electrons) come 4s² (20) and then 3d⁷ (27): [Ar] 4s² 3d⁷. [Ar] 3d⁹ skips 4s, which fills before 3d in a neutral atom; [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁸ invents an exception (only Cr- and Cu-type atoms do that); [Kr] is the noble gas AFTER cobalt; 4d belongs to period 5.",["Co is in period 4, the 7th element of the d-block.","Fill 4s before 3d in the neutral atom.","[Ar], then 4s², then seven d electrons."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q20"}],
["m7-q-003",7,13,2,"Exam","mcq","According to the aufbau order, which of these sublevels fills LAST?",["5p","6s","6p","4f","5d"],2,"The aufbau sequence in this region is … 5p → 6s → 4f → 5d → 6p. 6p comes after all of the others (it is the p-block of period 6, filled from Tl to Rn). 4f and 5d are filled before 6p even though 4f has a lower n; 5p and 6s come before 4f.",["Read the order off the periodic table row by row.","Period 6: 6s (Cs, Ba), then 4f (lanthanides), 5d, then 6p.","Which block is at the right-hand end of period 6?"],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q30"}],
["m7-q-004",7,13,2,"Exam","mcq","Which element has the ground-state electron configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴?",["O","P","S","Cl","Se"],2,"Add the superscripts: 2 + 2 + 6 + 2 + 4 = 16 electrons, so Z = 16: sulfur. Oxygen and selenium have the same OUTER pattern (ns² np⁴) but in shells 2 and 4; P has 3p³ and Cl has 3p⁵.",["Count the electrons.","For a neutral atom the electron count equals Z.","Z = 16: the third element of period 3’s p-block."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q31"}],
["m7-q-005",7,14,3,"Trap","mcq","What is the ground-state electron configuration of the Ni²⁺ ion?",["1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁸","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d⁶","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d⁸","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s¹ 3d⁷"],0,"Neutral Ni (Z = 28) is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁸. A transition metal loses its 4s electrons FIRST, so Ni²⁺ (26 electrons) is [Ar] 3d⁸ = 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁸. Keeping 4s² and removing two 3d electrons gives the classic wrong answer 4s² 3d⁶; 4s² 3d⁸ is the neutral atom; 3d¹⁰ has 28 electrons (that is Zn²⁺); 4s¹ 3d⁷ removes one electron from each.",["Start from neutral Ni and remove two electrons.","Transition metals lose ns electrons before (n−1)d electrons.","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁸ → remove both 4s electrons."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q27"}],
["m7-q-006",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq","Which gives the ground-state configuration of Co³⁺ and its number of unpaired electrons?",["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁴; 4 unpaired","[Ar] 3d⁶; 0 unpaired","[Ar] 3d⁶; 2 unpaired","[Ar] 3d⁶; 4 unpaired","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵; 6 unpaired"],3,"Co (Z = 27) is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁷. Remove three electrons, the two 4s electrons first and then one 3d: Co³⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁶. By Hund’s rule six electrons in five d orbitals go in singly first (5) and the sixth pairs up, leaving 4 unpaired - so the ion is paramagnetic. [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴ strips 3d before 4s; 0 unpaired pairs everything (ignores Hund’s rule); 2 unpaired pairs too early; 4s¹ 3d⁵ is a Cr-style pattern that does not apply to this ion.",["Remove 4s electrons before 3d electrons.","Co³⁺ has 24 electrons: [Ar] plus six d electrons. Then apply Hund’s rule.","Five orbitals, six electrons: one pair, four single."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q27"}],
["m7-q-007",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","Which set of species is isoelectronic (every member has the same number of electrons)?",["Li⁺, Na⁺, K⁺, Rb⁺","P³⁻, S²⁻, Cl⁻, K⁺","Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe","O²⁻, S²⁻, Se²⁻, Te²⁻","Ca²⁺, Sc²⁺, Ti²⁺, V²⁺"],1,"Electrons = Z − charge. P³⁻: 15 + 3 = 18; S²⁻: 16 + 2 = 18; Cl⁻: 17 + 1 = 18; K⁺: 19 − 1 = 18 - all 18 (the argon count). A shared charge or a shared group is not the same thing: Li⁺/Na⁺/K⁺/Rb⁺ have 2, 10, 18, 36; the noble gases have 10, 18, 36, 54; the group 16 anions 10, 18, 36, 54; Ca²⁺ 18, Sc²⁺ 19, Ti²⁺ 20, V²⁺ 21.",["Isoelectronic means the same NUMBER of electrons, not the same charge.","Electrons = atomic number − charge.","Look for a set that all reach 18."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q28"}],
["m7-q-008",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq","How many of the following have the ground-state configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶? Ar, K⁺, S²⁻, Ca²⁺, Cl, Ti⁴⁺",["1","3","4","5","6"],3,"That configuration holds 18 electrons. Count electrons (Z − charge): Ar 18 ✓, K⁺ 19 − 1 = 18 ✓, S²⁻ 16 + 2 = 18 ✓, Ca²⁺ 20 − 2 = 18 ✓, Cl 17 ✗ (neutral chlorine is 3p⁵), Ti⁴⁺ 22 − 4 = 18 ✓ (it loses both 4s and both 3d electrons). Five species. \"1\" counts only the noble gas; \"4\" usually misses Ti⁴⁺; \"6\" includes neutral Cl.",["Count the electrons in the target configuration.","For each species, electrons = Z − charge.","Only one species is not at 18."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q38"}],
["m7-q-009",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq","Which arranges these ions from SMALLEST radius (left) to LARGEST (right)? S²⁻, Cl⁻, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Sc³⁺",["K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < Sc³⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻","S²⁻ < Cl⁻ < K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < Sc³⁺","Sc³⁺ < Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < S²⁻ < Cl⁻","Cl⁻ < S²⁻ < Sc³⁺ < Ca²⁺ < K⁺","Sc³⁺ < Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻"],4,"All five have 18 electrons (isoelectronic), so size is set by nuclear charge: more protons pull the same 18 electrons in tighter. Z: S 16, Cl 17, K 19, Ca 20, Sc 21, so the order of increasing radius is Sc³⁺ < Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻. The fully reversed list ranks by Z the wrong way; \"K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < Sc³⁺\" thinks a higher charge makes a bigger cation; \"S²⁻ < Cl⁻\" at the end reverses the anions; the last list ranks the PARENT atoms and ignores the charges.",["Count electrons in each ion - what do you notice?","Same electron count: the ion with more protons is smaller.","Largest Z (Sc) smallest; smallest Z (S) largest."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q21"}],
["m7-q-010",7,14,3,"Trap","mcq","Which of these period 3 elements has the LARGEST first ionization energy? Mg, Al, Si, P, S",["Mg","P","Al","Si","S"],1,"IE₁ generally rises across a period, but there are two dips: group 13 is below group 2 (Al < Mg, the 3p electron is easier to remove than a 3s one) and group 16 is below group 15 (S < P). P is 3p³ - three half-filled 3p orbitals - while S (3p⁴) loses its one PAIRED electron, which is pushed out by electron-electron repulsion. Actual values: Mg 738, Al 578, Si 787, P 1012, S 1000 kJ/mol. Picking S follows the general trend but misses the dip.",["IE₁ rises across a period - but are there exceptions?","Compare the 3p³ and 3p⁴ configurations: which removes a paired electron?","Group 15 beats group 16."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q22"}],
["m7-q-011",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","Which element has the largest first ionization energy? Li, O, Sr, Sb, I",["Li","Sr","Sb","O","I"],3,"IE₁ increases toward the upper right of the table (up a group, across a period). O is in period 2, high on the right; I, Sb and Sr are in period 5, where the outer electrons are much farther from the nucleus; Li is an alkali metal with the lowest IE₁ of its period. Values: O 1314, I 1008, Sb 834, Sr 549, Li 520 kJ/mol. Choosing I (\"farthest right\") forgets that IE₁ also falls going DOWN a group.",["Two trends: across a period and down a group.","IE₁ is highest toward the upper right (He, F, O, N…).","Which choice is in period 2 on the right?"],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q33"}],
["m7-q-012",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq","An element in period 3 has these successive ionization energies (kJ/mol): IE₁ = 787, IE₂ = 1577, IE₃ = 3232, IE₄ = 4356, IE₅ = 16,091. Which element is it?",["Si","Mg","Al","P","S"],0,"Look for the big jump: IE₄ → IE₅ rises from 4356 to 16,091 kJ/mol (almost 4×), while the earlier steps are modest. So four electrons are valence electrons and the fifth comes from the core: a group 14 element. In period 3 that is Si ([Ne] 3s² 3p²). Mg would jump after IE₂, Al after IE₃, P after IE₅ and S after IE₆.",["Find where the ionization energies suddenly jump.","The number of electrons removed before the jump = number of valence electrons.","Jump after IE₄ → 4 valence electrons."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q23"}],
["m7-q-013",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","The first four ionization energies of an element are 419, 3052, 4420 and 5877 kJ/mol. In which group of the periodic table is the element most likely found?",["Group 1","Group 2","Group 13","Group 17","Group 18"],0,"The huge jump comes immediately: IE₁ = 419 but IE₂ = 3052 kJ/mol, over 7 times larger. Only ONE electron is easy to remove; the second comes from a noble-gas core. One valence electron = group 1 (these are potassium’s values). A group 2 element would jump after IE₂; groups 17 and 18 have large IE₁ values to begin with.",["Where is the first big jump?","Electrons removed before the jump = valence electrons.","IE₁ → IE₂ jumps, so one valence electron."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q34"}],
["m7-q-014",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq","The first three ionization energies of aluminum are 0.578, 1.817 and 2.745 MJ/mol. How much energy is needed to convert 2.40 mol of gaseous Al atoms into Al³⁺(g) ions?",["1.39 MJ","4.16 MJ","5.14 MJ","6.59 MJ","12.3 MJ"],4,"Al(g) → Al³⁺(g) + 3e⁻ removes all three valence electrons one after another, so the energy per mole is IE₁ + IE₂ + IE₃ = 0.578 + 1.817 + 2.745 = 5.140 MJ/mol. For 2.40 mol: 5.140 × 2.40 = 12.3 MJ. 1.39 MJ uses only IE₁ (makes Al⁺); 6.59 MJ uses only IE₃; 5.14 MJ is for one mole; 4.16 MJ assumes every electron costs IE₁.",["Making Al³⁺ means removing three electrons in turn.","Sum IE₁ + IE₂ + IE₃ (per mole), then multiply by the moles.","5.140 MJ/mol × 2.40 mol."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q35"}],
["m7-q-015",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","Which equation represents the THIRD ionization energy (IE₃) of scandium?",["Sc(g) → Sc³⁺(g) + 3e⁻","Sc³⁺(g) + e⁻ → Sc²⁺(g)","Sc⁺(g) → Sc²⁺(g) + e⁻","Sc²⁺(aq) → Sc³⁺(aq) + e⁻","Sc²⁺(g) → Sc³⁺(g) + e⁻"],4,"Each ionization energy removes ONE electron from a GASEOUS species, starting from the ion made in the previous step: IE₁ Sc → Sc⁺, IE₂ Sc⁺ → Sc²⁺, IE₃ Sc²⁺ → Sc³⁺. Sc → Sc³⁺ + 3e⁻ is the sum IE₁ + IE₂ + IE₃; the reverse equation (adding an electron) has the opposite sign; Sc⁺ → Sc²⁺ is IE₂; ionization energies are defined for gas-phase species, not aqueous ions.",["One electron per ionization step, from a gaseous species.","IE₃ starts from the ion made by IE₂.","Sc²⁺(g) loses one more electron."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q24"}],
["m7-q-016",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","Which element is the MOST metallic? Ga, Ge, As, Se, Br",["Ga","Ge","As","Se","Br"],0,"Metallic character increases toward the lower LEFT of the periodic table (easier to lose electrons). These five are all in period 4, so the leftmost, gallium (group 13), is the most metallic. Ge and As are metalloids; Se and Br are nonmetals.",["Where on the table are the most metallic elements?","Same period → moving left increases metallic character.","Which is furthest left?"],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q25"}],
["m7-q-017",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","For which pair of elements is the DIFFERENCE in metallic character greatest?",["Na and Cl","Cs and I","Cs and F","Li and F","Na and K"],2,"The most metallic elements are at the lower left (Cs, Fr) and the least metallic at the upper right (F, O). Cs and F sit at opposite corners, so their difference is largest. Na/Cl and Li/F span one period only; Cs/I are both in period 6; Na and K are both alkali metals.",["Metallic character: highest at the lower left, lowest at the upper right.","Pick the pair furthest apart in BOTH directions.","Opposite corners of the table."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q26"}],
["m7-q-018",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","Atomic radius shrinks steadily from Na to Cl across period 3. Which statement best explains this trend?",["The principal quantum number of the outer electrons increases across the period.","Shielding by inner electrons increases across the period, pulling the outer electrons inward.","The effective nuclear charge felt by the outer electrons increases across the period.","Adding electrons increases electron-electron repulsion, which compresses the atom.","The effective nuclear charge decreases across the period."],2,"Across a period each step adds one proton and one electron to the SAME shell (n = 3). The added same-shell electrons shield each other poorly, so Zeff on the outer electrons rises and pulls them in: the radius shrinks. n does not change within a period (it changes down a group); core shielding stays roughly constant across a period (always the [Ne] core); repulsion would make the atom larger, not smaller; and Zeff increases, not decreases.",["What changes, and what stays the same, from Na to Cl?","Same n for all the outer electrons; one more proton each step.","Effective nuclear charge (Zeff) rises."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q29"}],
["m7-q-019",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","Which lists these atoms in order of INCREASING atomic radius (smallest first)? K, Rb, Na, Al, P",["P < Al < Na < K < Rb","Rb < K < Na < Al < P","Na < Al < P < K < Rb","P < Al < Na < Rb < K","Al < P < Na < K < Rb"],0,"Radius decreases across a period and increases down a group. In period 3: P < Al < Na (P has the highest Zeff). Down group 1: Na < K < Rb (new shells). Together: P < Al < Na < K < Rb. The second list is the decreasing order; \"Na < Al < P\" runs the period trend backwards; \"Rb < K\" runs the group trend backwards; \"Al < P\" puts the group 13 atom ahead of the group 15 atom.",["Two trends: across a period and down a group.","Smaller to the right (Zeff), larger going down (more shells).","Start with P; finish with Rb."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q32"}],
["m7-q-020",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","For which property does chlorine have a LARGER value than bromine?",["atomic radius","number of protons","atomic mass","first ionization energy","radius of its X⁻ ion"],3,"Cl is above Br in group 17. Going down a group the outer electrons are in a higher shell, farther from the nucleus, so they are easier to remove: IE₁(Cl) = 1251 kJ/mol > IE₁(Br) = 1140 kJ/mol. Br is larger (atom and anion), has more protons (35 vs 17) and a larger atomic mass (79.90 vs 35.45).",["Cl and Br are in the same group - which one is higher?","Going down a group: size, Z and mass increase; IE₁ decreases.","The higher element has the larger IE₁."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q36"}],
["m7-q-021",7,14,2,"Exam","mcq","Which species has the LARGEST radius? O, O⁻, O²⁻, O⁺, F⁻",["O","O²⁻","O⁻","O⁺","F⁻"],1,"For the same nucleus (8 protons), adding electrons increases repulsion and the cloud swells: O⁺ < O < O⁻ < O²⁻. F⁻ has the same 10 electrons as O²⁻ but 9 protons instead of 8, so it is pulled in more tightly: O²⁻ > F⁻. O²⁻ is the largest.",["Same element: more electrons → larger.","Compare O²⁻ and F⁻: same electron count, different nuclear charge.","Fewer protons holding 10 electrons → bigger."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 2 (practice) Q37"}],
["m8-q-001",8,15,2,"Exam","mcq","In which pair are BOTH substances held together mainly by ionic bonding?",["NaCl and HCl","MgO and CO","SO₂ and K₂S","CH₄ and LiBr","CsF and BaO"],4,"Ionic bonding forms between a metal and a nonmetal with a large electronegativity difference. CsF (alkali metal + halogen) and BaO (alkaline earth metal + oxygen) are both ionic. In every other pair one member is made only of nonmetals and is covalent: HCl, CO, SO₂ and CH₄.",["Ionic: metal + nonmetal. Covalent: nonmetal + nonmetal.","Check BOTH compounds in each pair.","Find the pair where both contain a group 1 or 2 metal."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q3"}],
["m8-q-002",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Arrange KBr, CaO, NaF and SrS in order of INCREASING lattice energy (smallest first).",["NaF < KBr < CaO < SrS","KBr < NaF < SrS < CaO","CaO < SrS < NaF < KBr","KBr < SrS < NaF < CaO","SrS < CaO < KBr < NaF"],1,"Lattice energy ∝ (cation charge × anion charge)/(distance between ions). Charge matters most: KBr and NaF are 1+/1−, while SrS and CaO are 2+/2− (four times the charge product), so both 2+/2− salts beat both 1+/1− salts. Within each pair the smaller ions win: Na⁺ and F⁻ are smaller than K⁺ and Br⁻, so KBr < NaF; Ca²⁺ and O²⁻ are smaller than Sr²⁺ and S²⁻, so SrS < CaO. The lists that put SrS above CaO or KBr above NaF reverse the size effect; placing SrS between the 1+/1− salts lets size beat charge.",["Coulomb’s law: higher charges and smaller ions → larger lattice energy.","Sort by charge product first, then by ion size within each charge group.","1+/1− salts are smallest; within them the smaller ions come second."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q4"}],
["m8-q-003",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Taking lattice energy as ΔH for MX(s) → Mⁿ⁺(g) + Xⁿ⁻(g), arrange MgO, CsI, BaS and LiCl from SMALLEST to LARGEST lattice energy.",["LiCl < CsI < MgO < BaS","MgO < BaS < LiCl < CsI","CsI < BaS < LiCl < MgO","CsI < LiCl < BaS < MgO","BaS < MgO < CsI < LiCl"],3,"Charge first: CsI and LiCl are 1+/1−; BaS and MgO are 2+/2−, with a charge product four times larger, so they come last. Then size: Cs⁺ and I⁻ are much larger than Li⁺ and Cl⁻, so CsI < LiCl; Ba²⁺ and S²⁻ are larger than Mg²⁺ and O²⁻, so BaS < MgO. (Approximate values: CsI ≈ 600, LiCl ≈ 850, BaS ≈ 2800, MgO ≈ 3800 kJ/mol.) Putting BaS between the 1+/1− salts lets size outweigh charge; reversing either pair reverses the size effect.",["Lattice energy grows with ion charge and shrinks with ion size.","Group by charge product (1 or 4), then order each group by size.","Biggest ions with the smallest charges come first."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q11"}],
["m8-q-004",8,15,4,"Trap","mcq","Arrange Al₂O₃, CaF₂, KF and CaO in order of INCREASING lattice energy (energy to separate one formula unit’s worth, per mole, into gaseous ions).",["KF < CaF₂ < CaO < Al₂O₃","KF < CaO < CaF₂ < Al₂O₃","CaF₂ < KF < CaO < Al₂O₃","Al₂O₃ < CaO < CaF₂ < KF","KF < CaF₂ < Al₂O₃ < CaO"],0,"Look at the ion charges. KF is K⁺/F⁻ (charge product 1). CaF₂ has Ca²⁺ with F⁻ ions (product 2 per cation-anion contact). CaO is Ca²⁺/O²⁻ (product 4). Al₂O₃ is Al³⁺/O²⁻ (product 6, small ions, and five ions per formula unit). So KF < CaF₂ < CaO < Al₂O₃ (roughly 820, 2630, 3400 and 15,900 kJ/mol). Putting CaF₂ above CaO counts \"three ions\" as more important than the doubled anion charge; putting CaO above Al₂O₃ ignores the 3+ cation.",["Compare the charges on the ions in each compound.","Charge products: K⁺F⁻ 1, Ca²⁺F⁻ 2, Ca²⁺O²⁻ 4, Al³⁺O²⁻ 6.","The compound with the 3+ cation and 2− anion is largest."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q11"}],
["m8-q-005",8,15,2,"Exam","mcq","Which compound contains BOTH ionic and covalent bonds?",["KCl","CH₃OH","CaBr₂","N₂O₄","KNO₃"],4,"KNO₃ is made of K⁺ ions and NO₃⁻ ions held together ionically, but inside each nitrate ion the N and O atoms share electrons in covalent bonds. KCl and CaBr₂ are purely ionic (monatomic ions only); CH₃OH and N₂O₄ are purely covalent (nonmetals only).",["Look for a polyatomic ion.","A metal cation + a polyatomic anion: ionic between the ions, covalent inside the anion.","Nitrate."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q9"}],
["m8-q-006",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Use the data to find the lattice energy of KCl, defined as ΔH for KCl(s) → K⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g). ΔH°f of KCl(s) = −436.5 kJ/mol; ΔH sublimation of K(s) = +89.0 kJ/mol; IE₁ of K = +418.8 kJ/mol; Cl−Cl bond energy = 243.4 kJ/mol; electron affinity of Cl = −348.6 kJ/mol.",["−717.4 kJ","−155.6 kJ","+717.4 kJ","+839.1 kJ","+1414.6 kJ"],2,"Build K⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g) from KCl(s) by Hess’s law: KCl(s) → K(s) + ½Cl₂(g) (reverse formation, +436.5); K(s) → K(g) (+89.0); K(g) → K⁺(g) + e⁻ (+418.8); ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g) (½ × 243.4 = +121.7); Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g) (−348.6). Sum: 436.5 + 89.0 + 418.8 + 121.7 − 348.6 = +717.4 kJ. +839.1 uses the whole Cl−Cl bond energy (only ½ mol Cl₂ is needed); +1414.6 adds the electron affinity with the wrong sign; −155.6 forgets to reverse the formation step; −717.4 is the energy of FORMING the lattice, the opposite reaction.",["Write steps that turn KCl(s) into gaseous ions and add their ΔH values.","Reverse formation (+436.5), atomize K, ionize K, atomize ½Cl₂, add the electron affinity.","436.5 + 89.0 + 418.8 + 121.7 − 348.6."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q10"}],
["m8-q-007",8,15,3,"Exam","mcq","Gallium metal melts at about 30 °C (it melts in your hand) but does not boil until about 2400 °C. What best explains the enormous gap between its melting and boiling points?",["Gallium’s metallic bonds are weak, so it melts easily, and they strengthen as the liquid is heated.","Boiling gallium must break covalent Ga−Ga bonds that form only in the liquid.","Melting only lets the cations slide past one another within the electron sea; boiling must pull atoms completely out of the metallic bonding.","Gallium has fewer valence electrons than nonmetals, so its liquid holds onto them more tightly.","Melting breaks the metallic bonds, and boiling then breaks the stronger bonds between nuclei."],2,"In a metal the cations sit in a \"sea\" of delocalized valence electrons. Melting only loosens the regular arrangement - the cations can now flow past each other, but they are still held by the electron sea, so the metallic bonding is largely intact. Boiling must separate individual atoms from the sea entirely, which takes far more energy. That is why boiling points of metals are much higher than their melting points. Metallic bonds do not strengthen on heating, liquid gallium is still metallic, and melting does not break the metallic bonding.",["Picture the electron-sea model before and after melting.","Does melting separate atoms from each other, or just let them move?","Boiling has to break what melting leaves intact."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q17"}],
["m8-q-008",8,15,4,"Trap","mcq","Which statement about metals and metallic bonding is FALSE?",["The valence electrons are delocalized over the whole crystal.","Metals conduct electricity as solids because their delocalized electrons can move.","Melting a metal breaks most of its metallic bonding.","Metals are malleable because layers of cations can slide without breaking the electron sea.","For most metals the boiling point is far above the melting point."],2,"Melting does NOT break most of the metallic bonding: in the liquid the cations are still immersed in the delocalized electron sea and only their orderly arrangement is lost. That is why melting points are only moderately high while boiling points (where atoms leave the sea) are much higher. The other four statements describe the electron-sea model correctly.",["Find the one statement that contradicts the electron-sea model.","In liquid metal, are the atoms still sharing a sea of electrons?","Compare what melting and boiling each do to the bonding."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q17"}],
["m8-q-009",8,16,2,"Exam","mcq","Which of these carbon bonds is the SHORTEST?",["C−F","C−Cl","C−Br","C−I","C−S"],0,"Bond length is roughly the sum of the two atomic radii. Carbon is the same partner each time, so the shortest bond goes with the smallest partner atom: F, at the top of group 17 and far right of period 2. C−F (about 138 pm) < C−Cl < C−Br < C−I, and C−S (sulfur is a period 3 atom) is also much longer than C−F.",["Bond length tracks the sizes of the bonded atoms.","Same carbon each time - compare the partner atoms’ radii.","Smallest partner atom → shortest bond."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q5"}],
["m8-q-010",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","One mole of each compound is burned completely in oxygen to CO₂ and H₂O. Which releases the MOST heat?",["C₄H₁₀O","C₃H₈","C₄H₈O","C₄H₁₀","C₄H₁₀O₂"],3,"Combustion heat comes mostly from forming the very strong C=O and O−H bonds of CO₂ and H₂O. A fuel with more C and H makes more of them, and a fuel that already contains O is partly \"burned\": some of its C and H are already bonded to oxygen, so less energy is left to release. C₄H₁₀ has the most C and H (4 C, 10 H) and no oxygen, so it releases the most (about 2880 kJ/mol). C₃H₈ has one carbon fewer; C₄H₁₀O, C₄H₈O and C₄H₁₀O₂ already contain oxygen (and C₄H₈O has fewer H).",["Where does the heat of combustion come from?","More C and H → more CO₂ and H₂O formed; oxygen already in the fuel means it is partly oxidized.","Most C and H, no O."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q6"}],
["m8-q-011",8,16,4,"Exam","mcq","Rank these two-carbon compounds by the heat released per mole when each is burned completely, from LEAST to MOST: C₂H₆ (ethane), C₂H₅OH (ethanol), CH₃CHO (acetaldehyde), CH₃COOH (acetic acid).",["C₂H₆ < C₂H₅OH < CH₃CHO < CH₃COOH","CH₃COOH < CH₃CHO < C₂H₅OH < C₂H₆","CH₃CHO < CH₃COOH < C₂H₅OH < C₂H₆","CH₃COOH < C₂H₅OH < CH₃CHO < C₂H₆","C₂H₅OH < CH₃COOH < CH₃CHO < C₂H₆"],1,"All four make 2 CO₂ on burning. The more oxygen and the fewer hydrogens a compound already has, the more \"pre-oxidized\" its carbons are and the less energy remains to be released. C₂H₆ (no O, 6 H) releases the most; C₂H₅OH (one O, 6 H) less; CH₃CHO (one O, only 4 H) less again; CH₃COOH (two O) the least. Approximate values: 875, 1167, 1367 and 1560 kJ/mol. The reversed list ranks most to least; swapping ethanol and acetaldehyde misses that acetaldehyde has two fewer H atoms.",["Same number of carbons - so what differs is how oxidized each compound already is.","More O and fewer H in the fuel → less heat released.","Acetic acid least, ethane most."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q6"}],
["m8-q-012",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","Estimate ΔH for burning one mole of ethyne (acetylene, HC≡CH) to CO₂(g) and H₂O(g), using average bond energies (kJ/mol): C−H 413, C=C 614, C≡C 839, O=O 498, C=O in CO₂ 799, O−H 467. (Write the Lewis structures and balance the equation first.)",["−2440 kJ","−2059 kJ","−1445 kJ","−1220 kJ","+1220 kJ"],3,"Per mole of ethyne: C₂H₂ + 5/2 O₂ → 2CO₂ + H₂O. Bonds broken: one C≡C (839) + two C−H (826) + 2.5 O=O (1245) = 2910 kJ. Bonds formed: two CO₂ have four C=O (4 × 799 = 3196) and one H₂O has two O−H (934) = 4130 kJ. ΔH ≈ broken − formed = 2910 − 4130 = −1220 kJ. −2440 kJ is for the whole-number equation with 2 mol C₂H₂; −1445 kJ uses a C=C double bond (wrong Lewis structure); −2059 kJ forgets to break the carbon-carbon bond; +1220 kJ is formed − broken.",["Ethyne has a triple bond between the carbons.","ΔH ≈ Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed), per mole of C₂H₂ (5/2 O₂).","Broken 2910, formed 4130."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q7"}],
["m8-q-013",8,16,4,"Exam","mcq","Estimate ΔH for CH₃OH(g) + 3/2 O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g) using bond energies (kJ/mol): C−H 413, C−O 358, O−H 467, O=O 498, C=O in CO₂ 799.",["−1310 kJ","−1122 kJ","−1013 kJ","−655 kJ","+655 kJ"],3,"Methanol is H₃C−O−H: three C−H, one C−O and one O−H. Broken: 3(413) + 358 + 467 + 1.5(498) = 2811 kJ. Formed: CO₂ has two C=O (1598) and two H₂O have four O−H (1868) = 3466 kJ. ΔH ≈ 2811 − 3466 = −655 kJ. −1122 kJ forgets methanol’s own O−H bond; −1013 kJ forgets its C−O bond; −1310 kJ is for 2 mol of methanol; +655 kJ is formed − broken.",["Draw methanol: which bonds does it actually contain?","Broken: 3 C−H, 1 C−O, 1 O−H, 1.5 O=O. Formed: 2 C=O, 4 O−H.","2811 − 3466."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q7"}],
["m8-q-014",8,16,2,"Exam","mcq","Arrange these bonds in order of INCREASING polarity: Si−O, Cl−O, P−O, S−O.",["Si−O < P−O < S−O < Cl−O","S−O < Cl−O < P−O < Si−O","Cl−O < P−O < S−O < Si−O","P−O < S−O < Cl−O < Si−O","Cl−O < S−O < P−O < Si−O"],4,"Polarity follows the electronegativity difference ΔEN. Every bond includes O (3.5), so the partner with the LOWEST EN gives the most polar bond. EN increases across period 3: Si 1.8 < P 2.1 < S 2.5 < Cl 3.0, so ΔEN is 1.7 (Si−O), 1.4 (P−O), 1.0 (S−O), 0.5 (Cl−O). Increasing polarity: Cl−O < S−O < P−O < Si−O. The second list is the reverse - ranking by the partner’s EN instead of by the difference.",["Polarity depends on the electronegativity DIFFERENCE.","All four bonds share O; EN rises across period 3 from Si to Cl.","The partner closest to O in EN gives the least polar bond."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q8"}],
["m8-q-015",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","On the potential-energy curve for two H atoms, the energy reaches a minimum at the bond length (74 pm). If the nuclei are pushed even closer together than 74 pm, the energy rises steeply. Why?",["The electron-nucleus attractions grow faster than the repulsions, so the energy rises.","Nucleus-nucleus and electron-electron repulsions grow faster than the electron-nucleus attractions.","The H₂ molecule falls apart into separate H atoms, which have higher energy.","One electron transfers to the other atom, making an H⁺ H⁻ ion pair.","The atoms move apart again, so the attractions weaken."],1,"As the atoms approach from far away, each electron is attracted by both nuclei, and these attractions grow faster than the repulsions - the energy falls until the minimum at the bond length. Pushing closer than that, the two positive nuclei (and the two electrons) repel each other more and more strongly, and these repulsions now increase faster than the attractions, so the energy climbs steeply. Growing attractions would LOWER the energy; the atoms are not separating or transferring electrons in this region.",["What is balanced at the minimum of the curve?","Left of the minimum the nuclei are very close - which interaction dominates there?","Repulsions win at short distance."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q12"}],
["m8-q-016",8,16,2,"Exam","mcq","Using periodic trends, arrange the hydrogen halide bonds in order of DECREASING bond strength (strongest first).",["H−I > H−Br > H−Cl > H−F","H−Cl > H−F > H−Br > H−I","H−F > H−Br > H−Cl > H−I","H−F > H−Cl > H−Br > H−I","H−I > H−F > H−Cl > H−Br"],3,"Going down group 17 the halogen atom gets larger, so the bond to H gets longer and weaker (the shared electrons are farther from the halogen nucleus). Shortest and strongest is H−F (565 kJ/mol), then H−Cl (427), H−Br (363), H−I (295). The reversed list confuses bond strength with atom size; the other orders mix up the group sequence.",["Shorter bonds are stronger bonds.","Halogen size increases down group 17: F < Cl < Br < I.","Smallest halogen → strongest bond."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q13"}],
["m8-q-017",8,16,3,"Exam","mcq","Hydrogen peroxide could in principle form directly: H−H(g) + O=O(g) → H−O−O−H(g). Estimate ΔH using bond energies (kJ/mol): H−H 432, O=O 498, O−H 467, O−O 204.",["−2068 kJ","−208 kJ","−4 kJ","+208 kJ","+259 kJ"],1,"Broken: one H−H and one O=O = 432 + 498 = 930 kJ. Formed in H−O−O−H: two O−H and one O−O single bond = 2(467) + 204 = 1138 kJ. ΔH ≈ broken − formed = 930 − 1138 = −208 kJ. +208 kJ is formed − broken; −4 kJ forgets the new O−O bond; +259 kJ counts only one O−H; −2068 kJ adds everything with a minus sign.",["List every bond in the reactants and every bond in the product.","ΔH ≈ Σ(broken) − Σ(formed).","930 − (2 × 467 + 204)."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q14"}],
["m8-q-018",8,16,2,"Exam","mcq","Arrange barium (Ba), cesium (Cs), germanium (Ge) and selenium (Se) in order of INCREASING electronegativity.",["Cs < Ba < Ge < Se","Ba < Cs < Ge < Se","Se < Ge < Ba < Cs","Cs < Ba < Se < Ge","Ge < Se < Cs < Ba"],0,"Electronegativity increases to the right across a period and up a group. Cs (group 1, period 6) is the least electronegative of all (0.7), Ba (group 2, period 6) is next (0.9), then Ge (group 14, period 4, 1.8) and Se (group 16, period 4, 2.4). \"Ba < Cs\" reverses groups 1 and 2; \"Se < Ge\" reverses the p-block pair; the third list is the decreasing order.",["EN rises toward the upper right (F is highest).","Group 1 is lower than group 2; group 14 is lower than group 16.","Cs is the lowest; Se the highest."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q15"}],
["m8-q-019",8,16,3,"Trap","mcq","In which molecule are the bonds MOST polar? (Electronegativities: H 2.1, O 3.5, F 4.0, Cl 3.0, S 2.5, Se 2.4)",["OF₂","Cl₂O","H₂S","H₂Se","H₂O"],4,"Compare ΔEN for each bond: O−H 3.5 − 2.1 = 1.4; O−F 4.0 − 3.5 = 0.5; O−Cl 3.5 − 3.0 = 0.5; S−H 2.5 − 2.1 = 0.4; Se−H 2.4 − 2.1 = 0.3. The O−H bonds in water are the most polar. OF₂ is the trap: fluorine is the most electronegative element, but its partner oxygen is also very electronegative, so the DIFFERENCE is small.",["Bond polarity depends on the difference in EN, not on how electronegative one atom is.","Compute ΔEN for each bond.","O−H: 1.4."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q16"}],
["m9-q-001",9,17,3,"Exam","mcq","Acetic acid, CH₃COOH, has this skeleton: a CH₃ carbon bonded to a second carbon; that second carbon is bonded to two oxygen atoms, and one of those oxygens also carries the acidic H. In the best Lewis structure, how many lone pairs does the molecule have in total?",["2","3","4","5","6"],2,"Valence electrons: 2 C (8) + 4 H (4) + 2 O (12) = 24. Bonds: three C−H, C−C, C−O, O−H and the C=O double bond (needed to give the second carbon an octet) use 8 pairs = 16 electrons. The remaining 8 electrons are 4 lone pairs: two on the carbonyl O (=O) and two on the O−H oxygen. Neither carbon has lone pairs. 2 counts only one oxygen; 5 leaves the carbon-oxygen bond single and gives that O three lone pairs (the carbon is then left with only 6 electrons); 3 and 6 miscount the 24 electrons.",["Count the total valence electrons first.","Each oxygen ends up with two bonds’ worth of electrons; the second carbon needs a double bond.","Two lone pairs on each oxygen."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q18"}],
["m9-q-002",9,17,4,"Exam","mcq","Nitromethane, CH₃NO₂, has a CH₃ carbon bonded to N, and the N is bonded to both O atoms. In the best Lewis structure (every atom with an octet, H with 2), how many lone pairs are there in the whole molecule?",["4","5","6","7","8"],1,"Valence electrons: C 4 + 3 H 3 + N 5 + 2 O 12 = 24. Bonds: three C−H, C−N, one N=O and one N−O = 7 pairs = 14 electrons; 10 electrons remain = 5 lone pairs: 2 on the double-bonded O and 3 on the single-bonded O; N has none (it has 4 bonds and formal charge +1, the single-bonded O has −1). 6 comes from leaving both N−O bonds single (N would have only 6 electrons); 4 misses a lone pair on the single-bonded O; 7 and 8 put lone pairs on N, which needs more than 24 electrons.",["Total valence electrons: 24.","N needs a double bond to one O to complete its octet; then fill the oxygens.","Double-bonded O: 2 pairs; single-bonded O: 3 pairs; N: none."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q18"}],
["m9-q-003",9,17,3,"Exam","mcq","In the ICl₄⁻ ion the iodine atom is bonded to all four chlorine atoms. How many lone pairs are on the central iodine, and what is its formal charge?",["2 lone pairs; formal charge 0","3 lone pairs; formal charge −1","2 lone pairs; formal charge −1","1 lone pair; formal charge +1","4 lone pairs; formal charge −1"],2,"Valence electrons: I 7 + 4 Cl × 7 + 1 (the charge) = 36. Four I−Cl bonds use 8; each Cl takes 3 lone pairs (24); the remaining 4 electrons go on iodine as 2 lone pairs (an expanded octet, allowed for period 5). Formal charge on I = 7 − (4 lone-pair electrons + ½ × 8 bonding electrons) = 7 − 8 = −1, which also equals the ion’s overall charge since every Cl is 0. Forgetting the extra electron gives \"0\"; 3 lone pairs is the I₃⁻-style count with only two bonds.",["Count valence electrons including the 1− charge.","Give each Cl its octet, then put what is left on I.","FC = 7 − (lone-pair electrons + half the bonding electrons)."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q19"}],
["m9-q-004",9,17,4,"Trap","mcq","Diazomethane, CH₂N₂, has the skeleton H₂C−N−N (both H on carbon; the nitrogens in a chain). In its best Lewis structure, what are the formal charges on C, the middle N, and the end N, respectively?",["0, 0, 0","−1, +1, 0","0, +1, −1","+1, −1, 0","0, −1, +1"],2,"Valence electrons: C 4 + 2 H 2 + 2 N 10 = 16. Two candidates satisfy every octet: H₂C=N=N (C: 4 − 0 − 4 = 0; middle N: 5 − 0 − 4 = +1; end N with 2 lone pairs: 5 − 4 − 2 = −1) and H₂C−N≡N (C with a lone pair: 4 − 2 − 3 = −1; middle N +1; end N 0). Both have the same total charge separation, so the tie-breaker decides: the negative formal charge belongs on the MORE electronegative atom, N rather than C. Best: 0, +1, −1. \"0, 0, 0\" is impossible with 16 electrons and full octets; \"−1, +1, 0\" is the second-best structure; the last two put the charges on the wrong nitrogens.",["Count 16 valence electrons and try to give every atom an octet.","Two structures tie on the size of the formal charges - which rule breaks the tie?","Negative formal charge goes on the more electronegative atom."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q20"}],
["m9-q-005",9,17,3,"Exam","mcq","Fulminate, CNO⁻, is connected C−N−O (nitrogen in the middle). Which formal charge does that central N carry in the preferred Lewis structure?",["+2","+1","0","−1","−2"],1,"Valence electrons: C 4 + N 5 + O 6 + 1 = 16. The best structure is C≡N−O with one lone pair on C and three on O, giving formal charges C −1 (4 − 2 − 3), N +1 (5 − 0 − 4), O −1 (6 − 6 − 1); the charges sum to −1. The other octet structures, such as C=N=O (C −2, N +1, O 0), also put +1 on N, because the central N always has four bonds and no lone pairs. A neutral N (0) would need a lone pair on N, which the 16 electrons cannot supply while keeping every octet.",["16 valence electrons; N is the central atom.","With octets on C and O, how many bonds does N have, and any lone pairs?","Four bonds, no lone pairs: FC = 5 − 0 − 4."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q24"}],
["m9-q-006",9,18,2,"Exam","mcq","Propene is CH₂=CH−CH₃. What is the shape around each carbon atom, from left (CH₂) to right (CH₃)?",["trigonal planar, trigonal planar, tetrahedral","tetrahedral, tetrahedral, tetrahedral","linear, linear, tetrahedral","trigonal planar, tetrahedral, tetrahedral","bent, trigonal planar, tetrahedral"],0,"Count electron groups (each bond counts once, double bonds included): the CH₂ carbon has 2 C−H + 1 C=C = 3 groups → trigonal planar; the middle CH carbon has C−H, C=C and C−C = 3 groups → trigonal planar; the CH₃ carbon has 4 single bonds → tetrahedral. \"Linear\" would need a triple bond; counting the double bond as two groups gives the all-tetrahedral answer; \"bent\" requires a lone pair, which carbon here does not have.",["Count electron groups around each carbon; a double bond is one group.","3 groups → trigonal planar; 4 → tetrahedral.","Both carbons of the C=C have 3 groups."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q21"}],
["m9-q-007",9,18,2,"Exam","mcq","What is the molecular shape of the chlorate ion, ClO₃⁻?",["trigonal planar","tetrahedral","T-shaped","trigonal pyramidal","bent"],3,"Valence electrons: Cl 7 + 3 O × 6 + 1 = 26. Cl is bonded to three O atoms and keeps one lone pair: 4 electron groups, so the electron-group arrangement is tetrahedral, but the molecular shape (atoms only) is trigonal pyramidal, like NH₃. Trigonal planar ignores the lone pair; \"tetrahedral\" names the electron-group arrangement instead of the shape; T-shaped needs 5 groups; bent needs only two bonded atoms.",["Draw the Lewis structure: does Cl keep a lone pair?","3 bonding groups + 1 lone pair = 4 groups.","AX₃E shape."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q22"}],
["m9-q-008",9,18,3,"Exam","mcq","XeOF₄ has Xe bonded to one O and four F atoms. According to VSEPR, what are its electron-group arrangement and molecular shape?",["trigonal bipyramidal; seesaw","octahedral; square pyramidal","octahedral; octahedral","trigonal bipyramidal; trigonal bipyramidal","octahedral; square planar"],1,"Valence electrons: Xe 8 + O 6 + 4 F (4 × 7 = 28) = 42. Xe forms five bonds (one to O, four to F) and keeps one lone pair: 6 electron groups → octahedral arrangement. With one lone pair the atoms make a square pyramid (like BrF₅). \"octahedral; octahedral\" names the electron arrangement twice; the trigonal bipyramidal answers forget the lone pair (5 groups); square planar needs two lone pairs.",["Count bonds plus lone pairs on Xe; the Xe=O bond counts as one group.","6 groups → octahedral arrangement.","One lone pair in an octahedron."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q27"}],
["m9-q-009",9,19,3,"Trap","mcq","Which molecule has a bond angle of about 103-105°?",["SO₂","NH₄⁺","BrF₃","CS₂","OF₂"],4,"OF₂: O has two bonds and two lone pairs = 4 electron groups (tetrahedral arrangement), bent shape, and the two lone pairs squeeze the F−O−F angle below 109.5° to about 103°. SO₂ is also bent, but with only 3 electron groups (trigonal planar arrangement), so its angle is near 119° - \"bent\" alone does not tell you the angle. NH₄⁺ is a perfect tetrahedron (109.5°); BrF₃ is T-shaped (angles near 86° and 180°); CS₂ is linear (180°).",["The angle depends on the number of electron groups, not just the shape name.","4 groups with 2 lone pairs → bent, a little below 109.5°.","Like water."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q23"}],
["m9-q-010",9,19,4,"Exam","mcq","Arrange these molecules in order of INCREASING bond angle around the central atom: CO₂, H₂O, NH₃, CH₄, BCl₃.",["CO₂ < BCl₃ < CH₄ < NH₃ < H₂O","H₂O < NH₃ < CH₄ < BCl₃ < CO₂","NH₃ < H₂O < CH₄ < BCl₃ < CO₂","H₂O < NH₃ < BCl₃ < CH₄ < CO₂","CH₄ < NH₃ < H₂O < BCl₃ < CO₂"],1,"H₂O (4 groups, 2 lone pairs) ≈ 104.5°; NH₃ (4 groups, 1 lone pair) ≈ 107°; CH₄ (4 groups, no lone pairs) 109.5°; BCl₃ (3 groups) 120°; CO₂ (2 groups) 180°. More lone pairs squeeze the angle more, so H₂O < NH₃ < CH₄. The second list is decreasing; \"NH₃ < H₂O\" thinks one lone pair squeezes more than two; \"BCl₃ < CH₄\" thinks more atoms means a wider angle.",["Find the number of electron groups and lone pairs on each central atom.","Fewer groups → wider angle; among 4-group molecules each lone pair narrows the angle.","104.5° < 107° < 109.5° < 120° < 180°."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q23"}],
["m9-q-011",9,19,2,"Exam","mcq","In acetaldehyde, CH₃−CHO, what is the approximate H−C−H bond angle within the CH₃ group?",["90°","104.5°","109.5°","120°","180°"],2,"The CH₃ carbon has four single bonds (three C−H and one C−C) and no lone pairs: 4 electron groups, tetrahedral, about 109.5°. 120° belongs to the other carbon (C=O, 3 groups); 104.5° needs lone pairs (as in water); 90° and 180° are not tetrahedral angles.",["Count the electron groups on the CH₃ carbon.","Four single bonds, no lone pairs.","Tetrahedral."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q25"}],
["m9-q-012",9,19,2,"Exam","mcq","In acetaldehyde, CH₃−CHO, the second carbon is double-bonded to O and single-bonded to H and to the CH₃ carbon. What is the approximate O−C−H angle at this carbon?",["90°","104.5°","109.5°","120°","180°"],3,"The carbonyl carbon has three electron groups (C=O, C−H, C−C; a double bond counts once) and no lone pairs → trigonal planar, about 120°. 109.5° would need four groups (counting the double bond twice is the usual slip); 180° needs two groups.",["A double bond counts as ONE electron group.","Three groups around this carbon, no lone pairs.","Trigonal planar."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q26"}],
["m9-q-013",9,19,3,"Exam","mcq","Formic acid has the structure H−C(=O)−O−H: the carbon carries one H, a double-bonded O, and an O−H group. Predict the H−O−C angle at the O−H oxygen and the O=C−O angle at the carbon.",["about 109.5° and about 109.5°","about 120° and about 120°","about 180° and about 120°","about 105° and about 120°","about 105° and about 109.5°"],3,"The O−H oxygen has two bonds (to H and C) and two lone pairs: 4 electron groups, bent, with the angle squeezed below 109.5° to roughly 105° (like water). The carbon has three groups (C−H, C=O, C−O) and no lone pairs: trigonal planar, about 120°. Ignoring the oxygen’s lone pairs gives 180° (linear) or 120°; counting the C=O as two groups gives 109.5° at carbon.",["Treat each central atom separately: the O−H oxygen and the carbon.","Oxygen: 2 bonds + 2 lone pairs. Carbon: 3 groups.","Water-like angle at O; trigonal planar at C."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q28"}],
["m9-q-014",9,19,2,"Exam","mcq","Which one of these molecules is polar?",["NF₃","SiF₄","BCl₃","SF₆","CO₂"],0,"All five have polar bonds, so the shape decides. NF₃ is trigonal pyramidal (one lone pair on N), so the N−F dipoles do not cancel: polar. SiF₄ (tetrahedral), BCl₃ (trigonal planar), SF₆ (octahedral) and CO₂ (linear) are symmetric, so their bond dipoles cancel and the molecules are nonpolar.",["Polar molecule = polar bonds AND a shape that does not cancel them.","Which central atom has a lone pair?","A lone pair on N makes a pyramid."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 3 (practice) Q29"}],
["m12-q-001",12,26,3,"Exam","mcq","A 275.0 mL steel cylinder holds 0.0850 mol of helium and 0.2200 mol of argon at 42 °C. What is the total pressure in the cylinder?",["2.87 × 10⁻² atm","3.82 atm","7.99 atm","20.7 atm","28.7 atm"],4,"Dalton’s law: P(total) depends on the TOTAL moles of gas, n = 0.0850 + 0.2200 = 0.3050 mol. Use kelvin and litres: T = 42 + 273.15 = 315.15 K, V = 0.2750 L. P = nRT/V = (0.3050)(0.08206)(315.15)/(0.2750) = 28.7 atm. 2.87 × 10⁻² atm leaves V in mL; 3.82 atm uses 42 instead of kelvin; 20.7 atm and 7.99 atm are the partial pressures of argon and helium alone.",["Total pressure comes from the total moles of gas.","P = nRT/V with n = sum of moles, T in K, V in L.","(0.3050)(0.08206)(315.15)/0.2750."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q11"}],
["m13-q-001",13,28,2,"Exam","mcq","A radioactive nuclide decays by electron capture with a half-life of 8 hours. What fraction of the original nuclide is left after 32 hours, and what are the atoms it decayed into?",["1/4; atoms of a different element","1/16; atoms of a different element","1/16; a different isotope of the same element","1/32; atoms of a different element","1/8; a different isotope of the same element"],1,"32 h ÷ 8 h = 4 half-lives, so (½)⁴ = 1/16 remains. Electron capture turns a proton into a neutron: A stays the same but Z drops by 1, so the product is a DIFFERENT ELEMENT (one place to the left in the table). 1/4 divides 1 by the number of half-lives; 1/32 counts five half-lives; 1/8 counts three. A change in Z always means a new element, not a new isotope of the old one.",["How many half-lives fit into 32 hours?","Fraction left = (½)ⁿ. Electron capture changes Z by −1.","Four half-lives; Z changes."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q29"}],
["m13-q-002",13,28,3,"Exam","mcq","A zircon crystal contains 120 μmol of uranium-238 (t½ = 4.5 × 10⁹ yr) and 60.0 μmol of lead-206. If all the Pb-206 came from decay of U-238 in the crystal, how old is the crystal?",["1.8 × 10⁹ yr","2.6 × 10⁹ yr","3.0 × 10⁹ yr","4.5 × 10⁹ yr","7.1 × 10⁹ yr"],1,"Each Pb-206 atom was once a U-238 atom, so the crystal started with N₀ = 120 + 60.0 = 180 μmol of U and now has N = 120 μmol. First-order decay: t = (t½/ln 2) × ln(N₀/N) = (4.5 × 10⁹/0.693) × ln(1.50) = 2.6 × 10⁹ yr. 4.5 × 10⁹ yr uses N₀ = U and N = Pb (ratio 2 → exactly one half-life); 7.1 × 10⁹ yr treats the lead as the uranium remaining; 3.0 × 10⁹ yr assumes decay is linear in time; 1.8 × 10⁹ yr leaves out the ln 2 (uses k = 1/t½).",["What was the starting amount of U-238?","N₀ = U now + Pb now. Then ln(N₀/N) = kt with k = 0.693/t½.","ln(180/120) = ln 1.5."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q30"}],
["m13-q-003",13,28,2,"Exam","mcq","How does the mass of a ⁴He atom compare with the total mass of two ¹H atoms plus two free neutrons?",["It is exactly equal, because the same particles are present.","It is slightly greater, because energy had to be added to hold the nucleons together.","It is slightly less, only because the two ¹H atoms include electrons that ⁴He does not have.","It is about twice as large, because neutrons are much heavier than protons.","It is slightly less, because energy (the binding energy) was released when the nucleus formed."],4,"Two ¹H atoms + two neutrons contain the same particles as one ⁴He atom (2 p, 2 n, 2 e⁻). But when nucleons bind, energy is released, and by E = mc² the nucleus loses the corresponding mass: the mass defect (about 0.03 amu for ⁴He, ≈ 28 MeV). So ⁴He is slightly LIGHTER. \"Exactly equal\" ignores E = mc²; \"slightly greater\" has the energy flow backwards; ⁴He has two electrons too, so electrons are not the reason; a neutron is only about 0.1% heavier than a proton.",["Is energy released or absorbed when a nucleus forms from its nucleons?","Released energy carries away mass: Δm = ΔE/c².","The bound nucleus has a mass defect."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q31"}],
["m13-q-004",13,28,4,"Exam","mcq","Use these masses to find the binding energy per nucleon of lithium-7: ⁷Li atom 7.016004 amu, ¹H atom 1.007825 amu, neutron 1.008665 amu; 1 amu = 931.5 MeV.",["5.39 MeV/nucleon","5.61 MeV/nucleon","9.81 MeV/nucleon","13.1 MeV/nucleon","39.2 MeV/nucleon"],1,"⁷Li has 3 protons and 4 neutrons. Using ¹H ATOM masses keeps the electrons balanced (3 e⁻ on each side). Mass defect = 3(1.007825) + 4(1.008665) − 7.016004 = 0.042131 amu. Binding energy = 0.042131 × 931.5 = 39.2 MeV; per nucleon ÷ 7 = 5.61 MeV/nucleon. 39.2 is the TOTAL binding energy; 5.39 uses the bare proton mass 1.007276 amu with an atomic Li mass (electron mismatch); 13.1 divides by protons only; 9.81 by neutrons only.",["Count protons and neutrons in ⁷Li.","Δm = 3 m(¹H) + 4 m(n) − m(⁷Li); × 931.5 MeV/amu; ÷ number of nucleons.","0.04213 × 931.5 ÷ 7."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q31"}],
["m13-q-005",13,28,2,"Exam","mcq","When two light nuclei fuse into a heavier nucleus and energy is released, which statement is true?",["The number of nucleons decreases, which accounts for the energy released.","The number of nucleons is unchanged, but their total mass decreases.","The number of nucleons is unchanged, and their total mass increases.","The number of nucleons increases as the nuclei join.","Both the number of nucleons and the total mass are exactly unchanged."],1,"Nuclear equations conserve mass number: every proton and neutron going in comes out. What changes is how tightly they are bound. The product nucleus has a higher binding energy per nucleon, and the released energy appears as a small loss of mass (ΔE = Δm·c²). Nucleons are not destroyed or created; if the mass did not drop, no energy could be released.",["Mass number (nucleon count) is conserved in nuclear reactions.","The energy released comes from E = mc².","Same nucleons, less mass."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q32"}],
["m13-q-006",13,28,3,"Exam","mcq","When a uranium-235 nucleus captures a neutron and splits into two medium-mass nuclei plus a few neutrons, about 200 MeV is released. Which statement about the fission products is correct?",["Their binding energy per nucleon is lower than that of U-235.","They contain more nucleons in total than the U-235 and the neutron did.","Their binding energy per nucleon is the same; the energy comes from the kinetic energy of the neutron.","Their binding energy per nucleon is higher than that of U-235.","The energy comes from breaking chemical bonds in the uranium metal."],3,"Binding energy per nucleon peaks near iron-56. Nuclei as heavy as U-235 sit well below the peak, and medium-mass fission products sit closer to it, so their nucleons are more tightly bound. The extra binding energy is released, and the products have slightly less mass than U-235 + n. The total nucleon count is conserved (fission products + neutrons = 236); the incoming neutron is slow and brings almost no energy; chemical bonds involve only a few eV, millions of times smaller than 200 MeV.",["Where is the peak of the binding-energy-per-nucleon curve?","Moving toward that peak (from either side) releases energy.","Medium-mass products are more tightly bound."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q32"}],
["m13-q-007",13,28,2,"Exam","mcq","Binding energy per nucleon rises steeply from the lightest nuclei to a maximum near A = 56 (iron) and then falls slowly for heavier nuclei. Which nuclide is most likely to release energy by FUSING into a heavier nuclide?",["²⁰Ne","⁷⁵As","⁹⁸Mo","¹³⁸Ba","²³⁵U"],0,"Fusion releases energy only when the product is closer to the A ≈ 56 peak than the starting nuclei. ²⁰Ne is lighter than iron, so joining it with another light nucleus moves up the steep side of the curve. ⁷⁵As, ⁹⁸Mo and ¹³⁸Ba are already past the peak - making them heavier would move DOWN the curve and cost energy. ²³⁵U releases energy by fission, the opposite process.",["On which side of iron-56 does fusion release energy?","Fusion helps nuclei lighter than A ≈ 56; fission helps heavy ones.","Pick the only nuclide lighter than iron."],{"src":"2021 FALL Exam 1 (practice) Q33"}],
["m13-q-008",13,28,2,"Exam","mcq","Identify X in the fission reaction ²³⁵₉₂U + ¹₀n → ¹³⁷₅₂Te + X + 2 ¹₀n.",["⁹⁷₄₀Zr","⁹⁹₄₀Zr","⁹⁶₄₀Zr","⁹⁷₄₁Nb","⁹⁷₄₀Y"],0,"Balance mass numbers: 235 + 1 = 137 + A + 2(1) → A = 97. Balance atomic numbers: 92 + 0 = 52 + Z + 2(0) → Z = 40, which is zirconium: ⁹⁷₄₀Zr. ⁹⁹Zr forgets the two outgoing neutrons; ⁹⁶Zr forgets the incoming one; ⁹⁷₄₁Nb miscounts Z; Y is element 39, so \"⁹⁷₄₀Y\" pairs Z = 40 with the wrong symbol.",["Balance the top numbers and the bottom numbers separately.","A: 236 = 137 + A + 2. Z: 92 = 52 + Z.","Z = 40: which element?"],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q3"}],
["m13-q-009",13,27,3,"Exam","mcq","The stable isotopes of krypton (Z = 36) have mass numbers from 78 to 86. By which process is krypton-74 most likely to decay?",["beta (β⁻) decay","alpha decay","gamma emission only","neutron capture","positron emission or electron capture"],4,"Kr-74 has 36 protons and 74 − 36 = 38 neutrons (N/Z = 1.06). The stable isotopes have 42-50 neutrons, so Kr-74 is NEUTRON-POOR: it lies below the band of stability. It moves toward the band by turning a proton into a neutron, which positron emission and electron capture both do (Z − 1, A unchanged). β⁻ decay would make the shortage worse; alpha decay is for nuclei with Z > 83; gamma emission does not change N or Z; neutron capture is not a decay.",["Compare Kr-74’s neutron count with the stable isotopes’.","Too few neutrons (below the band) → convert p into n.","Which decays lower Z by 1 and keep A?"],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q4"}],
["m13-q-010",13,27,4,"Trap","mcq","Strontium’s stable isotopes have mass numbers 84, 86, 87 and 88. Which decay mode and product would you predict for strontium-93 (Z = 38)?",["positron emission, forming ⁹³Rb","β⁻ decay, forming ⁹³Rb","β⁻ decay, forming ⁹³Y","alpha decay, forming ⁸⁹Kr","electron capture, forming ⁹³Rb"],2,"Sr-93 has 93 − 38 = 55 neutrons, more than any stable Sr isotope (46-50): it is NEUTRON-RICH, above the band of stability. β⁻ decay turns a neutron into a proton: Z goes 38 → 39 and A stays 93, giving ⁹³Y. \"β⁻ forming ⁹³Rb\" moves Z the wrong way; positron emission and electron capture are for neutron-POOR nuclei; alpha decay is for Z > 83.",["Neutron-rich or neutron-poor compared with the stable isotopes?","Neutron-rich → β⁻ decay: Z + 1, A unchanged.","Z = 39 is yttrium."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q4"}],
["m13-q-011",13,27,3,"Exam","mcq","Iron-58 (⁵⁸₂₆Fe) can be converted into cobalt-59 (⁵⁹₂₇Co). Which sequence of processes accomplishes this?",["neutron capture, then α emission","neutron capture, then positron emission","neutron capture, then β⁻ emission","electron capture, then neutron capture","α emission, then β⁻ emission"],2,"A must rise by 1 (58 → 59) and Z by 1 (26 → 27). Neutron capture adds 1 to A: ⁵⁸Fe + ¹₀n → ⁵⁹₂₆Fe. Then β⁻ emission (n → p) raises Z by 1 at constant A: ⁵⁹₂₆Fe → ⁵⁹₂₇Co + ⁰₋₁β. α emission would lower A by 4; positron emission and electron capture LOWER Z (giving Mn); α then β⁻ gives ⁵⁴Mn.",["Track A and Z separately: what has to change?","A + 1 needs a neutron gained; Z + 1 at constant A is a β⁻ decay.","Capture first, then beta."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q12"}],
["m13-q-012",13,28,3,"Exam","mcq","Iodine-131 (t½ = 8.02 days) is used to treat thyroid disease. How long does it take a 50.0 mg sample to decay to 9.0 mg?",["2.47 days","16.0 days","19.8 days","24.1 days","44.6 days"],2,"Decay is first order: t = (t½/ln 2) × ln(N₀/N) = (8.02/0.693) × ln(50.0/9.0) = 11.57 × 1.715 = 19.8 days (about 2.5 half-lives: 50 → 25 → 12.5 → 9.0 sits between 2 and 3). 16.0 and 24.1 days round to a whole number of half-lives; 44.6 days scales t½ by 50/9 as if decay were linear; 2.47 is the number of half-lives, not days.",["9.0 mg is not a whole number of halvings from 50.0 mg.","ln(N₀/N) = kt with k = 0.693/t½.","t = 8.02 × ln(50.0/9.0)/0.693."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q18"}],
["m13-q-013",13,27,2,"Exam","mcq","Which one of these nuclides is stable?",["⁴⁰₂₀Ca","¹⁴₆C","²⁸₁₁Na","¹²₇N","²²²₈₆Rn"],0,"Ca-40 has 20 protons and 20 neutrons: N/Z = 1, right for a light nucleus, and both numbers are magic (20) and even. C-14 (8 n, 6 p) is neutron-rich (it is the radiocarbon dating isotope); Na-28 (17 n, 11 p) is far too neutron-rich; N-12 (5 n, 7 p) is neutron-poor; every nuclide with Z > 83, including Rn-222, is radioactive.",["For light nuclei the stable N/Z ratio is about 1.","Check N = A − Z for each, and remember Z > 83 is never stable.","Magic numbers 2, 8, 20, 28 help."],{"src":"2022 SPRING Exam 1 (practice) Q19"}],
]);
